addRun("a-second-judge-and-a-frontier-writer--writer-claude-haiku-5-5--harvey-labs--codex--employment-labor-draft-eeoc-position-statement", {"meta": {"benchmark": "harvey-labs", "harness": "codex", "model": "qwen3.8-27b", "task_title": "Draft EEOC Position Statement Responding to Race, Sex Discrimination, and Retaliation Charge", "axis": "minute", "set": "a-second-judge-and-a-frontier-writer", "arm": "writer-claude-haiku-5-5"}, "events": [{"id": "event_001", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_002", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_003", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_004", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_005", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_006", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_007", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_008", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_009", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_012", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_010", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_011", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_013", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_014", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_015", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_016", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_017", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_018", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_019", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_020", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_021", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_022", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_023", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_024", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_025", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_026", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_027", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_028", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_029", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_030", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_031", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_032", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_033", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_034", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_035", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_036", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_037", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_038", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_039", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_040", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_041", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_042", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_043", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_044", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_045", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_046", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_047", "status": "missed"}], "questions": [{"id": "question_01", "answer": "The agent's early attempt to use a tracked plan tool failed: a plan-update call came back as an unsupported tool (card_0004). It did not set up the tracked plan and proceeded without it, instead tracking its work in a file it wrote itself: process/notes.md, containing key facts, figures, a defense map for each allegation, and a list of source conflicts with the choice made for each.", "quote": "An early plan-update call failed as an unsupported tool (card_0004).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report's ordered phase table states that during early orientation \"An early plan-update call failed as an unsupported tool (card_0004),\" which matches card_0004: the update_plan call at turn 7 was rejected with \"unsupported call: update_plan.\" It does not give the 4-step plan contents or the exact error string, but those details are not required to convey the material answer: the tracked-plan attempt failed because the tool was unsupported, and the agent did not maintain a plan. The \"how did it proceed\" half is carried by the same phase table: phase 1 shows the agent moving into environment setup (workspace listing, pandoc/python-docx/openpyxl checks and pip installs, consistent with card_0005's mkdir process + tool checks), and phase 2 shows it continuing straight into reading the sources. A report-only reader can correctly conclude the agent proceeded without a tracked plan, which is exactly the point the gold answer protects.", "evidence": ["card_0004", "card_0005"]}, {"id": "question_02", "answer": "The agent installed python-docx and openpyxl because the source material it had to read included two spreadsheets (pip-tracking-log.xlsx and vpm-termination-log.xlsx) and seven Word documents, and its deliverable was a .docx letter. It established the packages were missing during its initial orientation step: it listed the workspace and found that pandoc was installed but python-docx and openpyxl were missing, then installed both with pip (cards 0001-0010) before moving on to the \"Reading the sources\" phase. The report does not record the specific command output or import error that revealed the absence, only that the agent \"found\" them missing while listing the workspace.", "quote": "1. Orientation 0001–0010 Listed the workspace and found pandoc installed but python-docx and openpyxl missing. Installed both with pip. An early plan-update call failed as an unsupported tool (card_0004).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report's orientation phase (cards 0001-0010, before \"Reading the sources\") states the agent \"found pandoc installed but python-docx and openpyxl missing\" and \"installed both with pip,\" and the follow-on phases show why those libraries were needed: the spreadsheets were \"read ... with openpyxl\" and the deliverable is a .docx built and checked with python-docx. This communicates the material trace-grounded answer - the install ran because the required Python libraries were absent, and their absence was established during orientation before any source was read. It matches cards_0005-0009 (pandoc/python3 present, combined and separate imports failing, pip install exiting 0). The report omits the specific verification mechanics - the which/import traceback and the pip-list grep plus separate import probes - and loosely ties the discovery to the workspace listing, but its account is carried by its own text and not contradicted, so the report-only reader could reconstruct the substantive answer (as it did).", "evidence": ["card_0005", "card_0006", "card_0007", "card_0008", "card_0009"]}, {"id": "question_03", "answer": null, "quote": null, "read": null, "reads": null, "verdict": "not_scored", "score": null, "rationale": null, "evidence": []}, {"id": "question_04", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer is a process story: the first inline pandoc dump (card_0011) was truncated before \"THE PARTICULARS ARE\"; the agent noticed the truncation (card_0012), then dumped just the charge to /tmp/charge.txt with pandoc, checked its size with wc -c (9427 bytes), and used sed to read the missed middle section, recovering the allegations in full (card_0013). The report never tells this story. It contains no mention of the charge dump being truncated before the allegations, no /tmp/charge.txt, no 9427-byte size check, and no sed-based recovery of the middle section. The only relevant passages are generic gestures at truncation that do not materially answer the question: \"Several outputs were cut off by the tool, so the agent re-read line ranges and searched with grep.\" (in the Reading the sources phase row) and \"The agent opened all of them, though not all in a single read.\" / \"Several reads were cut off by the tool, so some sources were read in several passes.\" These say outputs were cut and re-read, but not that the first charge dump stopped before the allegations, nor how the full charge text was obtained (file dump + size check + targeted re-read of the middle), and they are not tied to the recovered \"THE PARTICULARS ARE\" content. The report does surface some facts that came from the charge's allegations (e.g., the $7,269.23 severance, the Jan 17, 2025 termination), but only as draft-position rows sourced to card_0013/card_0054, not as what the recovery read produced. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0011", "card_0012", "card_0013"]}, {"id": "question_05", "answer": "The agent created a working notes file at process/notes.md in the workspace (i.e. /app/workspace/process/notes.md, since the workspace's output file is /app/workspace/vpm-position-statement.docx). It started writing it as it read the sources (phase \"3. Fact notes\", cards 0015, 0054, 0058, within the reading phase 0011-0056) and recorded key facts, figures, a defense map for each allegation, and a list of source conflicts with the choice made for each. A verification log was later appended to the notes during the final checks.", "quote": "3. Fact notes 0015, 0054, 0058 Wrote process/notes.md : key facts, figures, a defense map for each allegation, and a list of source conflicts with the choice made for each.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks what working arrangement the agent set up under /app/workspace/process and what it recorded there. Card_0014 shows the agent deciding to keep a process note alongside moving on to the nine remaining documents, and card_0015 shows the actual creation of /app/workspace/process/notes.md containing the task definition, key transmittal facts (due date, investigator, the disclosure rule that the body is visible to the charging party with sensitive data in a confidential attachment, 20-day response window), charge particulars, and the documents-to-review list, with later cards appending verified facts. The report communicates this materially: its phase table says the agent \"Wrote process/notes.md: key facts, figures, a defense map for each allegation, and a list of source conflicts with the choice made for each\" (citing card 0015 among others), its final-checks row shows the notes were sustained by appending a verification log, the environment-changes item states the agent \"created process/notes.md in the workspace,\" and the disclosure bullet attributes the disclosable-body/confidential-attachment rule to the transmittal guidance recorded via card_0015. The report's content summary emphasizes the accumulated state of the ledger (facts, defense map, conflicts) rather than enumerating the initial task/transmittal/review-list sections verbatim, and its phase ordering does not stress that the file was created before the reading phase, but the core trace-grounded answer — a persistent working notes file under process/ used as the fact ledger for the drafting work — is clearly conveyed and supported by the report's own text, so this is answerable.", "evidence": ["card_0014", "card_0015"]}, {"id": "question_06", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks why the agent dumped every workspace .docx to plain-text files under /tmp/src before its detailed review. The trace (card_0016, card_0017, card_0018) shows the inline pandoc read of the three key supporting documents produced 22,951 tokens and was truncated mid-stream, so the agent reasoned \"The output is truncated partway through... Let me dump each document to a text file and carefully review each one\" and then created /tmp/src and pandoc-dumped all seven .docx files (136,635 bytes total). The report never mentions /tmp/src, a dump step, or the truncated multi-document read as the trigger; a grep of the report source finds no \"/tmp\", \"src\", or \"dump\". It only gestures at the topic without answering the why: the phase table says \"Converted each Word file to text with pandoc and read the spreadsheets with openpyxl. Several outputs were cut off by the tool, so the agent re-read line ranges and searched with grep.\" and the caveats say \"Several reads were cut off by the tool, so some sources were read in several passes\". These are vague topic mentions of conversion and truncation, but the report does not state the specific truncated output from reading the three supporting documents inline, the decision to dump each document to a text file, or the /tmp/src location, so the material answer is absent.", "evidence": ["card_0016", "card_0017", "card_0018"]}, {"id": "question_07", "answer": "The agent used \"Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.\" as the respondent name because the EEOC charge and the EEOC transmittal letter use that name, while several internal HR documents use other names (Vanguard, Valiant).", "quote": "Respondent name. It used “Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.” because the charge and the EEOC transmittal use that name, and several internal documents use other names (cards 0039, 0054).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report explains both the conflict and the reason for the name choice: internal documents carry different letterheads (Vanguard on the review form, PIP and EEO policy, Valiant on the termination memo, vanguardprecision.com email domains) while the EEOC charge and transmittal use Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc., so the draft names the respondent Saxonbrook. This matches card_0039 (deciding to use the EEOC complaint/cover-letter name), card_0053 (treating the charge/transmittal as the authoritative EEOC documents and planning to note the discrepancy), and card_0054 (recording the explicit DECISION in process/notes.md to use Saxonbrook + VPM throughout). The report also says the choice was recorded in process/notes.md as part of \"a list of source conflicts with the choice made for each,\" which carries the gold answer's point about the recorded decision.", "evidence": ["card_0039", "card_0053", "card_0054"]}, {"id": "question_08", "answer": "The agent verified the severance figure ($7,269.23) stated in the EEOC charge by recomputing the arithmetic behind it — four weeks of pay, $94,500 divided by 52 times 4 — as part of its mechanical checking of the draft. The outcome was that the severance arithmetic was recomputed and found consistent, so the charge's figure was confirmed as accurate and used in the draft.", "quote": "Arithmetic. Severance, percentage reductions and day intervals were recomputed and found consistent (cards 0058, 0081, 0084).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent verified the $7,269.23 severance figure stated in the EEOC charge before using it, and the outcome. Card_0051 shows the agent recomputing the claim against the final salary ($94,500/52 = $1,817.31/week; 4 weeks = $7,269.23, \"the calculation checks out\"), and card_0054 shows the figure carried into the verified fact base in process/notes.md. The report conveys this material answer in three places: the \"Severance and backfill\" row labels $7,269.23 as the \"Charge figure (card_0013)\" and gives the cross-check formula ($94,500 divided by 52, times 4); the \"How the work was checked\" list states severance was \"recomputed and found consistent\"; and the claims table marks the severance figure as \"Visible in charge text; arithmetic checked.\" It does not quote the phrase \"the calculation checks out\" or the intermediate $1,817.31, but the method (recomputing the charge's arithmetic against the $94,500 salary) and the outcome (confirmed consistent and used) are clearly communicated and match the trace. The report-only reader's extraction agrees and was verifiable in the report.", "evidence": ["card_0051", "card_0054"]}, {"id": "question_09", "answer": "The agent resolved the four-way conflict by its own choice: it cited the ID from the formal review compilation (prepared at counsel's request, the form ending in 0247), placing that ID only in the confidential attachment CA-5. It treated the mismatches as a noted source conflict — the four records giving IDs ending 0247, 0284, 0847 and 1087 — recorded in its conflict list with the choice made for each, and settled it without raising it with the user.", "quote": "Employee ID. It used the ID from the formal review compilation prepared for counsel and placed it only in the confidential attachment (cards 0054, 0060).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report answers both halves of the question. It states the decision — the cited ID is the one from the formal review compilation prepared for counsel (the 0247 form) — and its handling of the mismatches: the four records giving four different IDs (0247, 0284, 0847, 1087) are documented as a source conflict, not attributed to agent error, and reliance on the ID was minimized by placing it only in the confidential attachment. This matches the trace: card_0029 shows the 0247/0284 mismatch first flagged while reading the PIP, card_0053 shows the weighing (use the ID from the formal personnel-file compilation prepared at counsel's request, keep it minimal), and card_0054 records the explicit DECISION (cite VPM-2019-0247, minimize; treat variants as source inconsistencies). The report omits the per-document source mapping of each variant and the word \"DECISION,\" but it communicates the material trace-grounded answer; the report-only reader's extraction is accurate and both quotes locate verbatim.", "evidence": ["card_0029", "card_0053", "card_0054"]}, {"id": "question_10", "answer": "The agent did not resolve the conflict in favor of either document: in the position statement it dated the comparator Sellers' performance coaching plan only as \"during 2024,\" sidestepping the investigation summary's July 2024 date and the termination log's 11/01/2024. The report lists this as one of several source-conflict choices the agent made on its own, without raising them with the user.", "quote": "The Sellers coaching-plan date conflicts. The investigation summary dates his coaching plan to July 2024. The termination-log note gives 11/01/2024. The draft says only “during 2024” (cards 0047, 0054).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report states both sides of the date conflict (investigation summary: July 2024; termination-log note: 11/01/2024) and explains how the position statement handled it: it says only \"during 2024,\" deliberately not pinning the unreconciled date. A separate item also frames the Sellers date as one of the choices the agent made on its own. This matches card_0053 and card_0054, where the agent records an explicit DECISION to say \"during 2024\" (true per both sources) and avoid the exact date. The report does not spell out that the plan was informal/non-PIP or that Sellers was later terminated 2/7/25 for failing coaching objectives, but the question asks how the agent handled the date disagreement in the position statement, and the report answers that completely. The report-only reader's extraction is accurate.", "evidence": ["card_0053", "card_0054"]}, {"id": "question_11", "answer": "The agent produced a single Word file, /app/workspace/vpm-position-statement.docx, structured so the letter body stayed disclosable while the sensitive material (comparator data, witness accounts, company-wide statistics) was moved into a confidential attachment — attachments CA-1 to CA-5 — inside that same single file, a split between disclosable body and confidential attachments following the transmittal's guidance. This satisfies the single-output-file constraint while keeping sensitive material marked not for disclosure to the charging party.", "quote": "The agent produced one Word file, /app/workspace/vpm-position-statement.docx . It is a letter to the EEOC Atlanta District Office, dated April 18, 2025, that answers each allegation and asks for dismissal. Comparator data, witness accounts and company-wide statistics sit in a separate section marked not for disclosure to the charging party.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent reconciled the single-output-file constraint with the transmittal's requirement to keep sensitive material out of the charging party's hands. The report answers it directly and consistently across several places: it says one .docx was produced, that the disclosable letter body was kept separate from a confidential attachment section inside that same file marked not for disclosure to the charging party, that this split followed the transmittal's guidance, and that the attachment set is CA-1 to CA-5 (with the 2022-2025 pattern/termination data identified as CA-4 content in the draft-arguments table). This matches the structure decision recorded in process/notes.md on card_0053/card_0054 (body, then a marked confidential attachment holding comparator data, witness accounts, and the termination/replacement logs). The report omits some specifics from the gold — the explicit page break, the exact \"CONFIDENTIAL ATTACHMENT - NOT FOR DISCLOSURE TO CHARGING PARTY\" heading text, and the per-CA mapping — but the material trace-grounded answer (in-file, clearly marked confidential attachment holding the sensitive material, referenced from a disclosable body, all in one file) is clearly communicated and carried by the report's own text, which it corroborates with the 14 structural checks including the confidential marking (card_0082, echoed in the report's caveats section).", "evidence": ["card_0053", "card_0054"]}, {"id": "question_12", "answer": "The source-reading phase (cards 0011-0056) ended when the agent had opened all 11 source files in the workspace (seven Word documents, two emails, two spreadsheets), re-reading line ranges and grepping wherever tool outputs were cut off. At that point, before starting the draft (which began at card 0059), the agent produced the fact-notes file process/notes.md, containing the key facts and figures, a defense map for each allegation, and a list of source conflicts with the choice made for each.", "quote": "The workspace held 11 source files: seven Word documents, two emails and two spreadsheets (card_0002). The agent opened all of them, though not all in a single read.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The gold answer (card_0053: the agent declared a full picture of the sources after reading the last source, the EEO policy, and began synthesizing; card_0054: the append of the verified fact base, document outline, and inconsistency resolutions to /app/workspace/process/notes.md, ending the chunk) is materially communicated by the report. Its ordered phase table marks the source-reading phase as cards 0011-0056 (covering the EEO policy reads at cards 52 and 56, which the materials table also ties to that file), followed by the \"Fact notes\" phase in which the agent wrote process/notes.md containing the key facts and figures (the verified fact base: ratings, transition, comparators, complaint/investigation timeline, PIP objectives and results, termination approvals, severance/replacement, pattern statistics), and a list of source conflicts with the choice made for each (the inconsistency resolutions), before the \"Drafting\" phase begins at card 0059. The report does not quote the agent's \"I now have a full picture\" declaration or name the deliverable outline by that word, but the outline is reflected in the drafting row's planned structure, and the substantive content of the answer - when reading ended and what was produced in notes.md before drafting - is carried by the report's own text. The report-only reader's extraction is accurate and both of its quoted passages locate verbatim.", "evidence": ["card_0053", "card_0054"]}, {"id": "question_13", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer is a specific pre-drafting step: per card_0055 the agent decided to check the still-unread tail of the HR investigation summary and the EEO policy's retaliation/complaint-procedure sections (4.3, 5.1, 5.2) \"so I can cite it accurately,\" card_0056 shows it doing exactly that (sed lines 95-130 of the investigation summary plus grep of the policy), and card_0057 closes the phase with \"Great, I have everything. Let me write the script.\" I searched the full report (including all staged text); it never identifies the remaining unread parts checked before the script (the investigation summary's findings-of-fact tail, the policy's retaliation and complaint-procedure provisions) nor quotes or paraphrases the agent's stated next step (\"After that, I'll write the build script\" / \"Let me write the script\"). The report only gestures at the topic: it says \"Several outputs were cut off by the tool, so the agent re-read line ranges and searched with grep\" and, in its caveats, \"the EEO policy&rsquo;s retaliation section was read separately (cards 0052, 0055, 0056),\" and it lists a \"4. Drafting\" phase (cards 0059-0060) in which the Python script was written. Notably, the report explicitly discards the source of the answer: \"Cards 53 and 57 are mostly the agent&rsquo;s long reasoning text, so this report relies on its commands, file writes and tool outputs rather than on that reasoning.\" These are vague topic mentions that do not materially answer which unread parts were checked or what the agent said it would do next, so the answer is missing from the report. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is consistent.", "evidence": ["card_0055", "card_0056", "card_0057"]}, {"id": "question_14", "answer": "The core of the anti-retaliation defense was to tie each allegation to a documented, legitimate reason and to anchor the argument on the supervisor's December 18, 2023 email documenting Harmon's performance deficiencies, which predates her September 12, 2024 complaint (the protected activity). To rebut the comparators named in the charge, the draft: (1) on the claim that no similarly situated male was placed on a PIP, cited a 2022 PIP given to a CNC Operator II in a different position (terminated January 2023), acknowledging the position differs and the PIP predates the charged period but using it to show PIPs are not unique to Harmon; and (2) on the Leadership Development Program non-nomination, pointed out the program requires 18 months under the current supervisor, that Harmon had only about 11 months, and that neither comparator was nominated either. The comparator data, witness accounts and company-wide statistics were held in a separate confidential section marked not for disclosure to the charging party.", "quote": "Defense structure. It tied each allegation to a documented reason, and anchored the argument on the supervisor’s December 18, 2023 email, which predates the September 12, 2024 complaint (card_0058).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report conveys the material trace-grounded answer. It states the core strategy: a per-allegation defense tying each of the charge's claims to documented, legitimate reasons, anchored on the pre-complaint timeline (deficiencies documented in the Dec 18, 2023 supervisor email before the Sept 12, 2024 complaint; the FY2024 review 6 days before the complaint per the timeline; three-level termination approval; company-wide pattern data of 16 performance terminations and 5 formal PIPs across races and sexes). It also explains the comparator rebuttals: for the LDP non-nomination, the facially neutral 18-month tenure rule with Harmon at ~11 months and neither comparator nominated; for the \"no similarly situated male on a PIP\" claim, the Grady 2022 PIP with the agent's own caveats that the position differs and the PIP predates the charged period. This matches the defense map the agent wrote in card_0057/card_0058 (timeline anti-retaliation case plus rating/tenure/PIP comparator rebuttals). The report-only reader's extraction is consistent with the report and is itself supported.", "evidence": ["card_0057", "card_0058"]}, {"id": "question_15", "answer": "The agent structured the single .docx with a disclosable letter body plus a confidential attachment because it was following the EEOC transmittal letter's confidentiality guidance: it kept the letter body disclosable and moved sensitive material (comparator data, witness accounts, company-wide statistics) into confidential attachments CA-1 to CA-5 marked not for disclosure to the charging party.", "quote": "Disclosure. It kept the letter body disclosable and moved sensitive material into a confidential attachment, following the transmittal’s guidance (cards 0015, 0059).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report communicates the material trace-grounded answer: the single deliverable is one Word file in which a disclosable letter body is paired with a separate section/attachments (CA-1 to CA-5) holding comparator data, witness accounts and company-wide statistics, deliberately marked not for disclosure to the charging party, and the stated reason is the EEOC transmittal letter's confidentiality guidance. That matches the \"Structure decision (single .docx per task req, honoring EEOC confidentiality guidance)\" recorded in the agent's notes (card_0057, card_0058). The report's phase table also shows the drafting plan included the confidential attachments CA-1 to CA-5 in the same .docx. It omits two secondary details from the gold answer — that the one-file structure follows a single-output-file task constraint with a page break between parts, and that the agent chose not to formally assert privilege to block EEOC review — but neither omission contradicts the report, and the report is not merely a vague gesture: it names the structure, the marking, and the reason. The report-only reader's extraction is consistent with the report; no miss to note.", "evidence": ["card_0057", "card_0058"]}, {"id": "question_16", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent decided who signs the position statement and what it did about outside counsel. Per card_0057 and card_0058, the agent's notes record: \"Milliken sig + counsel Blackwell Thayer & Oakes\", the letterhead \"On behalf of\" the company, Sandra Milliken (VP of Human Resources) as the named transmittal representative and member of the termination approval chain, and a firm-level counsel block with no individual attorney name because none is named in the source records. The report does not communicate this. It never names Sandra Milliken, never names the firm Blackwell, Thayer & Oakes, and never says the signatory was taken from the transmittal/approval chain rather than invented or that the agent deliberately kept counsel at firm level to avoid fabricating an attorney name. Its \"Why the agent made its main choices\" section lists other choices (respondent name, disclosure, defense structure, employee ID, date arithmetic) but not the signatory or counsel decision. The report only gestures at the topic in a caveat: \"The letter is written for the signature of VPM&rsquo;s Vice President of Human Resources, with outside counsel named.\" That is a passing title-level mention without the reasoning or the counsel handling the question asks about, so the material answer is absent. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0057", "card_0058"]}, {"id": "question_17", "answer": "The agent used \"Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.\" because the EEOC charge and the EEOC transmittal letter use that name, while several internal documents (the review form, the PIP and the EEO policy use \"Vanguard\"; the termination memo uses \"Valiant\") use other names. The draft follows the name in the charge and transmittal rather than the internal records, and the report flags the mismatch, recommending that the legal name be confirmed because the exhibits carry the other names.", "quote": "Respondent name. It used “Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.” because the charge and the EEOC transmittal use that name, and several internal documents use other names (cards 0039, 0054).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report explains the name choice with the same substance as the trace: the agent used \"Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.\" because the EEOC charge and transmittal - the authoritative EEOC documents for a response to the charge - use that name, while the internal documents carry conflicting variants. The first quote gives the decision and its basis; the second enumerates the conflicting internal variants (Vanguard in the review form/PIP/EEO policy, Valiant in the termination memo) and confirms the draft uses Saxonbrook. This matches the decision recorded in process notes per card_0058 (\"use legal name 'Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.' (per charge/transmittal), 'VPM' abbrev.\") and the card_0057 context. The report adds a caveat to confirm the legal name, which is faithful to the record and does not contradict the explanation. The report does not quote the exact notes line or mention \"VPM\" as the chosen abbreviation, but it materially answers why the agent made the choice, so it is answerable.", "evidence": ["card_0057", "card_0058"]}, {"id": "question_18", "answer": "The agent verified the $7,269.23 severance figure by checking it against the charge text and by recomputing the underlying arithmetic: the figure is \"four weeks of pay,\" derived as $94,500 divided by 52, times 4, and the severance arithmetic was \"recomputed and found consistent\" (cards 0058, 0081, 0084). The report marks the claim's status as \"Visible in charge text; arithmetic checked\" (card_0013, card_0054). That verification shaped the defense by letting the draft cite the charge's own figure, restated with its arithmetic, and position it as an offer made on condition of a release that Harmon declined — with the release status and the March 10, 2025 replacement hire drawn from card_0054.", "quote": "Severance and backfill Four weeks of pay, $7,269.23 ($94,500 divided by 52, times 4), was offered on condition of a release and was declined. A replacement was hired March 10, 2025 at $97,000. Charge figure (card_0013); release status and hire (card_0054).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent verified $7,269.23 before citing it and how that verification shaped the severance defense. The report conveys the material answer: the figure is shown as the recomputation $94,500 divided by 52, times 4 (four weeks of her final salary), and the report's checking section states that severance arithmetic was recomputed and found consistent, citing card_0058 — the card in which the agent recorded the cross-check in its notes. The report ties the figure to the charge text (card_0013) and its status row reads \"Visible in charge text; arithmetic checked.\" It then presents the defense shaped by that verification: the draft frames the severance allegation as four weeks of pay offered on condition of a release that Harmon declined, alongside the March 10, 2025 replacement hire — i.e., a routine 4-week severance offer, matching what cards_0057 and card_0058 show (\"$7,269.23 = 94,500/52*4 ... Confirmed = charge + term log\" and \"Severance/replacement -> routine lawful severance (declined); normal backfill\"). Minor omissions (the explicit \"term log\" cross-check and the \"routine lawful / E-7 backfill\" phrasing) do not change the substance: the verification and its use in the defense are both communicated and carried by the report's own text.", "evidence": ["card_0057", "card_0058"]}, {"id": "question_19", "answer": "After reading and cross-checking the sources (the \"Reading the sources\" phase, cards 0011-0056), the agent first wrote fact notes to process/notes.md — key facts, figures, a defense map for each allegation, and a list of source conflicts with the choice made for each. It then moved into a \"Drafting\" phase (cards 0059-0060) in which it planned a letter with numbered sections, lettered exhibits, and confidential attachments CA-1 to CA-5, and wrote one Python script containing the full text and ran it to produce the .docx.", "quote": "3. Fact notes 0015, 0054, 0058 Wrote process/notes.md : key facts, figures, a defense map for each allegation, and a list of source conflicts with the choice made for each.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks (1) how the agent transitioned into authoring and (2) what complete document structure it had planned before writing the generator script. Card_0059 shows the transition (\"Alright, let's build the document\") and the full pre-write plan: EEOC Atlanta addressee (Attn: Investigator Strickland), April 18, 2025 date, body sections I-VI, exhibits A-J, confidential attachments CA-1..CA-5 on a separate page, and a Milliken/with-counsel signature block; card_0060 shows that plan embodied in /tmp/build_ps.py. The report communicates this in substance: its phase table credits cards 0059-0060 with planning \"a letter with numbered sections, lettered exhibits, and confidential attachments CA-1 to CA-5\" and writing/running one Python script producing the .docx, and elsewhere it places the letter with the EEOC Atlanta District Office dated April 18, 2025, the Exhibit A-J plus CA-1 to CA-5 list, the confidential \"not for disclosure\" separation, and the VP of HR signature with outside counsel. The report omits the verbatim \"Alright, let's build the document\" opening, the named section titles I-VI, the individual exhibit descriptions, and the investigator's name (the report never mentions \"Strickland\"), so it is less granular than the gold answer; but the material, trace-grounded answer - that authoring began only after a complete pre-planned architecture was settled, and what that architecture was at the structural level - is faithfully carried by the report's own text. A reader gets the transition and the planned structure, so this is answerable.", "evidence": ["card_0059", "card_0060"]}, {"id": "question_20", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "Card_0059 records the agent's stated fidelity rule (do not propagate source inconsistencies, do not add details absent from the record) and its three specific applications: (a) avoid mixing CAR No. 2024-IA-017/022 with the tracking log's AUD-2024-xxx numbers, citing the PIP for overdue-as-of-PIP items and the log for final outcomes; (b) for NAF-2024-NC-0042 (Lot #KW-2024-4471), rephrasing the January 7 verification / January 10 completion conflict as completed by the January 15, 2025 customer-imposed due date and verified in a January 2025 follow-up audit; (c) listing counsel as the firm Blackwell, Thayer & Oakes LLP without naming an attorney because no attorney name appears in the record. The report contains none of this. It has no CAR No., AUD-, NAF-, or lot-number strings, no mention of the follow-up audit, and no statement of a fidelity rule; \"Blackwell, Thayer & Oakes\" never appears. The report only gestures at the general theme of source conflicts (e.g., \"The agent settled several conflicts in the source documents by its own choices and did not raise them with the user\", \"a list of source conflicts with the choice made for each\", and \"Several choices were made without asking. These include the respondent name, the employee ID, the Sellers date, ... and the framing of the investigation as thorough and in good faith (card_0059)\"), and mentions only that the draft is signed \"with outside counsel named.\" Those are vague topic mentions about different conflicts (company name, employee ID, Sellers date, backfill count); none materially answers the stated rule or its application to (a), (b), or (c). The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0059"]}, {"id": "question_21", "answer": "The agent produced the EEOC position statement by planning a letter with numbered sections, lettered exhibits, and confidential attachments CA-1 to CA-5, then writing one Python script containing the full text and running it to produce /app/workspace/vpm-position-statement.docx (after first installing python-docx with pip). It confirmed the file mechanically rather than legally: text extraction (261 lines of extracted text; python-docx opens it with 132 paragraphs), about 30 spot-checked strings (charge number, key figures, names, a citation) whose counts were re-run after later edits, recalculated arithmetic, and 14 structural checks covering addressee, charge number, each allegation, the confidential marking, the exhibit list and the signature block, all of which passed, plus confirmation that no \"Vanguard\" or \"Valiant\" text remained. During verification it fixed several errors it found, mostly wrong date intervals, and reworded an ambiguous PIP-target sentence; a verification log was appended to its notes and a closing message declared the work \"complete and verified.\" That step matters for everything that followed because the checked .docx is the single deliverable the whole record turns on: the report's caveats (an unreviewed draft in a company's name with no counsel review, conflicting backfill figures, conflicting company names and employee IDs, a possibly stale April 18, 2025 date, and exhibits that are an index only) are all properties of that one draft, and the closing \"complete and verified\" claim rests on it. The report's bottom line is that the evidence supports a draft that was mechanically checked, but not a legal verification.", "quote": "The agent produced one Word file, /app/workspace/vpm-position-statement.docx . It is a letter to the EEOC Atlanta District Office, dated April 18, 2025, that answers each allegation and asks for dismissal.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The gold answer (card_0060) is that the docx was machine-generated: the agent wrote a python-docx script embedding the full statement text (letterhead, body, attachments) and ran it, producing /app/workspace/vpm-position-statement.docx successfully, and that this file was the artifact every later verification and fix operated on. The report communicates this material answer: its phase table states the agent \"Wrote one Python script containing the full text and ran it to produce the .docx\" (phase 4, cards 0059-0060), and its phase 5 row plus \"How the work was checked\" section show extraction, spot-checks, recomputed arithmetic, and 14 structural checks all run against that file, with fixes applied over several rounds and a verification log appended. The bottom line also names the single deliverable, /app/workspace/vpm-position-statement.docx. Fine details from the card (script name /tmp/build_ps.py, heredoc mechanism, \"saved\" exit output, patch-and-rerun of the same script) are not spelled out, but the report's own text carries the substance: machine-generated by one embedded-text Python script, confirmed by checks against the resulting file, with later repairs operating on it. The report-only reader's extraction matches what the report says, so no extraction miss to note.", "evidence": ["card_0060"]}, {"id": "question_22", "answer": "Verification began in the \"Verification and repair\" phase by opening the freshly built file with pandoc and python-docx, then counting key strings and re-doing the arithmetic. The first verification command failed because the expected tools were not installed: the `file` and `xxd` tools were missing from the environment, so the agent fell back on python-docx instead.", "quote": "Opened the file with pandoc and python-docx, counted key strings, and re-did the arithmetic.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent began verifying the freshly built docx and why that first verification command failed. The trace (card_0061-0063) shows the first attempt was `cd /app/workspace && file vpm-position-statement.docx && pandoc ... | head -60`, which failed with exit code 127 because the `file` binary is not installed (\"file: command not found\"), so the `&&`-chained pandoc preview never ran. The report does address how verification began, but gets it wrong: it says the agent began by \"Opened the file with pandoc and python-docx,\" presenting a successful opening rather than the failed file-and-pandoc command, and it never states that the first verification command failed (no exit code, no \"command not found\", no note that pandoc never executed). It does correctly note that the `file` tool was not installed (citing card_0062, the failed command), but attaches that fact to the wrong consequence — a fallback to python-docx — instead of the failure of the first verification command. The report's reader extraction mirrors this misreading. Because the report addresses the question with an account contradicted by its own cited cards, it is unsupported_or_contradicted.", "evidence": ["card_0061", "card_0062", "card_0063"]}, {"id": "question_23", "answer": "Because the `file` and `xxd` tools were not installed, the agent worked around it by relying on python-docx instead: it opened the .docx with python-docx. That workaround only established that python-docx could parse/open the file (132 paragraphs, with the extracted text running to 261 lines) — i.e., that the file was readable/parseable — not that its format was verified through a real file-type check.", "quote": "The extracted text runs to 261 lines, and python-docx opens the file with 132 paragraphs (cards 0066, 0070, 0085). The file and xxd tools were not installed, so the agent relied on python-docx instead (cards 0062, 0064).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent worked around the missing `file` binary to confirm the docx is a valid Office document, and what that workaround established. The trace (card_0063, card_0064) shows the immediate workaround: a zip magic-byte attempt (`head -c 4 vpm-position-statement.docx | xxd | head -1`) chained with a pandoc parse; xxd was also missing, so no byte-level check was ever obtained, and the only positive signal was that pandoc parsed and rendered the document (Saxonbrook header, April 18, 2025, EEOC Atlanta addressee, charge number). The report addresses this exact situation but misstates it: it says the agent \"relied on python-docx instead (cards 0062, 0064)\" and frames the workaround's establishment as python-docx opening the file with 132 paragraphs. Cards 0062 and 0064 show `file: command not found` and the xxd+pandoc attempt, not python-docx, which enters the record later in the verification pass (cards 0066/0070/0085). The report never mentions the magic-byte (head -c 4 | xxd) attempt, the second failure, or that pandoc's parse was the actual workaround, and it never states what the workaround actually established (pandoc could parse the document, not a file-type confirmation). Its separate note that \"the agent described the readability check as 'the test the evaluator uses' (card_0065)\" gestures at the pandoc parse but does not connect it to the missing `file` binary. The report's explanation is carried by neither the trace nor its own cited cards.", "evidence": ["card_0063", "card_0064"]}, {"id": "question_24", "answer": "Since the `file` and `xxd` tools were not installed, the agent's validity test for the docx was that python-docx could open the file (132 paragraphs), with the extracted text running to 261 lines. It then adopted a mechanical verification plan: text extraction, about 30 spot-checked strings (charge number, key figures, names, a citation, re-run after edits), recalculated arithmetic (severance, percentage reductions, day intervals), a handful of exact-match quotation checks, and 14 structural checks (addressee, charge number, each allegation, confidential marking, exhibit list, signature block), all of which passed.", "quote": "The extracted text runs to 261 lines, and python-docx opens the file with 132 paragraphs (cards 0066, 0070, 0085). The file and xxd tools were not installed, so the agent relied on python-docx instead (cards 0062, 0064).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks which validity test the agent settled on once `file` and `xxd` were unavailable, and what verification plan followed. The trace (card_0065) shows the agent settled on pandoc parsing the docx without issues — \"that's the test the evaluator uses\" — and then committed to a full systematic pass: extracting the complete plain text to /tmp/ps_out.txt and cross-checking a checklist of key facts, figures, dates, names, and citations (card_0066). The report's verification-plan half is substantially right (it describes the 261-line text extraction and ~30 spot-checked strings of charge number, figures, names, and citations), but its validity-test answer contradicts the trace: it says \"the agent relied on python-docx instead\" after `file` and `xxd` were missing. python-docx opening the file was a separate, later check (card_0070, turn 73), not the test settled on at turns 68–69, which was pandoc's successful parse. The report's own citation of \"the test the evaluator uses\" garbles the point further, relabeling it a \"readability check\" without ever naming pandoc as the validity test. Since the report addresses the question but misattributes the settled validity test, it is unsupported_or_contradicted rather than answerable.", "evidence": ["card_0065", "card_0066"]}, {"id": "question_25", "answer": "It established that the generated position statement is a readable, well-formed document whose full text contains the expected key facts. The extraction confirmed the file opens and yields 261 lines of text / 132 paragraphs, and the key-fact spot-check confirmed that about 30 strings — the charge number, key figures, names, and a citation — are present in the extracted text, with counts re-run after later edits. Overall these checks passed, and the process surfaced and led to fixes of several errors, mostly wrong month intervals.", "quote": "The agent checked the file mechanically: text extraction, about 30 spot-checked names and figures, recalculated arithmetic, and 14 structural checks, all of which passed. It fixed several errors it found along the way, mostly date intervals.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report states the two material findings from card_0066: the full-text extraction yielded 261 lines (matching the wc -l result of 261 /tmp/ps_out.txt), and the grep spot-check of roughly 30 key strings — charge number, key figures, names, citation — found them all present, with the checks passing and feeding the correction pass (\"re-run after later edits\", \"fixed several errors it found\"). The report omits the per-pattern counts and the nuance that the hyphenated \"18-month\" had 0 hits while \"eighteen\" appeared 4 times, but that detail is a refinement of the headline result, not the core answer, and nothing in the report contradicts the card.", "evidence": ["card_0066"]}, {"id": "question_26", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report only gestures at this part of the fact-check pass; it does not give the answer. Its \"Verification and repair\" phase row says the agent \"Found wrong month intervals and fixed them over several rounds; one leftover phrase took three attempts (cards 0077&ndash;0080). Reworded a sentence about the PIP target that read as ambiguous.\", and the \"Date arithmetic\" bullet says it \"corrected &ldquo;five months&rdquo; and &ldquo;nine months&rdquo; after computing the December 18, 2023 to September 6, 2024 interval as about 8.6 months (cards 0067, 0068, 0074, 0080).\" That is consistent with the month-math checks in card_0067 and the four fixes in card_0068, but it is not the answer to the question: the report never identifies the four issues the agent decided to correct (the three separate month-interval rewordings in II.E, III.E.1 and IV, plus the ambiguous PIP-target sentence), and it never says how the corrections were applied — the report is silent on the trace-grounded fact that the agent patched the generator script /tmp/build_ps.py with str.replace() calls and re-ran the builder to regenerate the .docx (the report only notes, in an earlier phase, that the .docx was produced by one Python script). The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is directionally right: the specific compound answer (which four issues, and the script-patch-plus-rebuild method) is absent, leaving only a topic gesture that does not materially answer the question.", "evidence": ["card_0067", "card_0068"]}, {"id": "question_27", "answer": "The report does not use the phrase \"four text patches,\" but it records how the agent confirmed its text edits were in the regenerated file: after fixing the errors (wrong month intervals fixed \"over several rounds,\" a leftover phrase that \"took three attempts,\" and a reworded PIP-target sentence), the agent re-extracted the regenerated .docx with pandoc and python-docx and re-ran its string spot checks — \"About 30 strings were counted in the extracted text, including the charge number, key figures, names and a citation. The counts were re-run after later edits (cards 0066, 0084)\" — alongside re-done arithmetic and 14 structural checks that all passed.", "quote": "About 30 strings were counted in the extracted text, including the charge number, key figures, names and a citation. The counts were re-run after later edits (cards 0066, 0084).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent confirmed that the four text patches had actually taken effect in the regenerated position statement. Per card_0068, the trace-grounded answer is a rebuild-plus-grep: the agent patched /tmp/build_ps.py (four replacements; the script printed \"patched\"), re-ran it (\"saved\"), re-extracted vpm-position-statement.docx to plain text with pandoc into /tmp/ps_out.txt, and grepped the extracted text for the corrected strings (\"more than eight months\" found at lines 71 and 109, \"more lenient than the Company\" at line 111), so the corrected draft was accepted — with the blind spot that grepping only the new strings did not reveal that the section IV \"nine months earlier\" replacement had silently not matched, which surfaced later (cards 0077–0080).\n\nThe report does not communicate this. The report-only reader's answer (\"re-extracted the regenerated .docx with pandoc and python-docx and re-ran its string spot checks\") overstates what the report says and is not itself the answer: the reader stitched together Phase 5, the \"How the work was checked\" section, and the 14 structural checks, none of which describe confirming the four patches took effect.\n\nIn the report, the only verbatim mentions of this step are generic. Phase 5 says: \"Opened the file with pandoc and python-docx, counted key strings, and re-did the arithmetic. Found wrong month intervals and fixed them over several rounds; one leftover phrase took three attempts (cards 0077&ndash;0080). Reworded a sentence about the PIP target that read as ambiguous.\" The check section says: \"About 30 strings were counted in the extracted text, including the charge number, key figures, names and a citation. The counts were re-run after later edits (cards 0066, 0084).\" and \"The extracted text runs to 261 lines, and python-docx opens the file with 132 paragraphs (cards 0066, 0070, 0085).\" These describe the generic spot-check regime (the ~30-string count of card_0066, card_0084's final log) and that the wrong month intervals were fixed over several rounds, but they are vague topic mentions that do not materially answer the question: no rebuild of the generator script, no targeted grep for the corrected strings in the regenerated file, and no indication that the targeted check missed one silently-failed replacement — the exact verification procedure and its blind spot that card_0068 shows. Hence missing_from_report.", "evidence": ["card_0068"]}, {"id": "question_28", "answer": "After the first round of text patches, the agent entered a verification and repair phase (cards 0061–0081). That pass consisted of opening the .docx with pandoc and python-docx, counting key strings, and re-doing the arithmetic; it found wrong month intervals and fixed them over several rounds (one leftover phrase took three attempts), and reworded a sentence about the PIP target that read as ambiguous.", "quote": "5. Verification and repair 0061–0081 Opened the file with pandoc and python-docx, counted key strings, and re-did the arithmetic. Found wrong month intervals and fixed them over several rounds; one leftover phrase took three attempts (cards 0077–0080). Reworded a sentence about the PIP target that read as ambiguous.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks what new phase the agent entered after the first round of text patches and what that pass consisted of. Cards 0069 and 0071 show the answer: after declaring the document \"renders cleanly,\" the agent ran a structured, fact-by-fact re-verification of the draft against the source documents (re-confirming 287 employees, the Kennesaw address and (770) 555-0312 phone, the EEOC Atlanta address, Poole's \"ballooned to 23.4 business days\" quote, the $7,269.23 severance, the 16 terminations and demographics, the 5 PIPs, the backfill figures, the 1/13-1/17/2025 approval chain, the 35-day gap, the 4-business-day investigation start, EEO policy sections 4.3 and 5, and the April 18 deadline) and then planned the closeout: confirm all 7 charge allegations were addressed, re-review the middle sections (II.F through V), and finalize the process notes. The report addresses the question but characterizes the phase differently and incompletely: it folds cards 0061-0081 into a single \"Verification and repair\" row describing mechanical file checks (pandoc/python-docx, counting key strings, re-doing arithmetic, fixing month intervals, rewording a PIP sentence), frames verification as \"checked the file mechanically ... spot-checked names and figures,\" and states there was \"no sentence-by-sentence comparison of the draft against the sources.\" Nothing in the report conveys the item-by-item re-confirmation of key figures against the sources, the 7-allegation coverage check, the re-review of middle sections II.F-V, or the closeout of the process notes, and its \"no comparison ... against the sources\" claim is not carried by the trace, which shows the agent re-confirming facts against the source documents one by one. The report-only reader's extraction faithfully reflects the report's own (mis)characterization, but the report does not communicate the trace-grounded answer.", "evidence": ["card_0069", "card_0071"]}, {"id": "question_29", "answer": "The workbook's Summary Statistics tab counts 13 completed backfills (race 7 Caucasian, 2 African American, 2 Hispanic, 2 Asian American; 8 male, 5 female), while its Replacement Hires tab states a total of 12 completed backfills (race 7/2/2/1; 8 male, 4 female) even though its rows list 13 completed hires, including the Harmon replacement (record 14). The agent resolved the conflict by taking the Summary Statistics tally over the Replacement Hires tab, so CA-4 states 13 as fact. The report notes this choice was made without asking the user, flags the final checklist's citation of the Replacement Hires tab for the 13 figure as a miscitation, and recommends reconciling against HR records before filing.", "quote": "The Summary Statistics tab counts 13 backfills (race 7 Caucasian, 2 African American, 2 Hispanic, 2 Asian American; 8 male, 5 female). The Replacement Hires tab of the same workbook states a total of 12 completed backfills (race 7/2/2/1; 8 male, 4 female), but its rows list 13 completed hires, including the Harmon replacement (record 14). The agent’s own row count gave 8 Caucasian hires, so the race splits conflict as well (card_0069). The final checklist cites the Replacement Hires tab for the 13 figure (card_0081), which is a miscitation. CA-4 states 13 as fact.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report answers both halves of the question. First, the conflicting numbers: it gives the Summary Statistics tab's 13 completed backfills (7 Caucasian, 2 African American, 2 Hispanic, 2 Asian American; 8 male, 5 female) against the Replacement Hires tab's stated 12 (7/2/2/1; 8 male, 4 female), and even notes the tab's own rows count to 13 — matching card_0069, where the agent discovers the same discrepancy. Second, the resolution: the report says CA-4 \"states 13 as fact\" and lists \"the choice of the Summary Statistics tally over the Replacement Hires tab\" among the agent's conscious choices, which matches the card showing the agent weighing softer language and keeping the internally consistent, directly traceable Summary Statistics figures. The report's own conflict table repeats the 13-vs-12 statement. The card_0069 trace shows the agent's rationale was traceability to an authoritative, internally consistent tab; the report conveys the substance of that decision (kept the Summary Statistics figures in CA-4) even without quoting the rationale verbatim, so the answer is materially complete and well supported.", "evidence": ["card_0069"]}, {"id": "question_30", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer (cards 0070, 0071) is a specific step: the agent re-opened vpm-position-statement.docx with python-docx, confirmed it opened cleanly with 132 paragraphs, then printed the tail of the rendered plain text from \"INDEX OF DISCLOSED EXHIBITS\" onward to confirm the exhibit index (A-J) and confidential attachments (CA-1, CA-4, CA-5) rendered properly, and only then, satisfied the document was complete, moved on to review the middle sections (II.F-V) not yet verified. The report never describes the tail-rendering check, never names \"INDEX OF DISCLOSED EXHIBITS\", never mentions the rendered-text tail as verification, and never mentions the middle-sections review at all (the words \"render\", \"tail\", and \"middle\" appear nowhere in the report). Its verification section covers extraction, spot checks, arithmetic, quotations, and 14 structural checks, none of which is the tail-rendering verification the question asks about. The report does gesture at one facet of the answer - \"python-docx opens the file with 132 paragraphs (cards 0066, 0070, 0085)\" - but that vague topic mention does not materially answer how rendering was verified before the middle sections were reviewed, so the answer is missing. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT verdict is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0070", "card_0071"]}, {"id": "question_31", "answer": "During its verification-and-repair pass the agent re-did the month arithmetic, computing the December 18, 2023 to September 6, 2024 interval as about 8.6 months, which showed the \"five months\" (and \"nine months\") phrasing was wrong; it corrected the phrases, fixing the month-interval errors over several rounds — one leftover phrase took three attempts (cards 0077–0080).", "quote": "Found wrong month intervals and fixed them over several rounds; one leftover phrase took three attempts (cards 0077–0080).", "read": "complete", "reads": 2, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report addresses the month-math cleanup but does not faithfully convey how the residual \"five months\" error was discovered and repaired. Cards 0072-0074 show the agent read lines 40-108 of the re-rendered text (/tmp/ps_out.txt), manually spotted that Section III.A.4 still said \"five months\" (Dec 18, 2023 -> Sep 6, 2024 is ~8.6 months), patched build_ps.py with an asserted replacement (\"five months\" -> \"more than eight months\"), rebuilt, re-rendered, and verified by grep (zero \"five months\" remaining, 3 occurrences of \"more than eight months\"). The report's \"Date arithmetic\" bullet instead attributes the correction of \"five months\" (and \"nine months\") to the agent's computation of the 8.6-month interval, omitting entirely the discovery-by-reading-the-rendered-output step, the Section III.A.4 location, the asserted patch, and the grep verification; that is the core of the question. It also misattributes the \"leftover phrase ... took three attempts\" (cards 0077-0080) to the round in general, when the trace shows the \"five months\" fix succeeded in a single asserted pass (card_0074) while the three-attempt struggle was the differently worded \"nine months\" instance in section IV. The 8.6-month figure the report states is consistent with the cards, but the report as written is not carried by the trace for how the residual \"five months\" error was found and fixed, so it gets it wrong rather than merely vague. Note: the report-only reader's second attempt located these two passages correctly; the first attempt's NOT_IN_REPORT was a miss.", "evidence": ["card_0072", "card_0073", "card_0074"]}, {"id": "question_32", "answer": "Because the phrase was a wrong date-interval statement that the agent's mechanical checks did not catch in the first passes: during verification it recomputed the December 18, 2023 to September 6, 2024 interval as about 8.6 months, which exposed the incorrect \"nine months\" (and \"five months\") wording, and the correction had to be applied over several rounds — the leftover phrase took three attempts before it was fixed (cards 0077–0080). The diagnosis was the arithmetic re-check of month intervals (cards 0067, 0068, 0074, 0080), and the fix was targeted at those month-interval phrases, ultimately replacing the \"nine months\" interval.", "quote": "Opened the file with pandoc and python-docx, counted key strings, and re-did the arithmetic. Found wrong month intervals and fixed them over several rounds; one leftover phrase took three attempts (cards 0077–0080).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks why section IV still said \"nine months earlier\" after both patch rounds (the first patch's Fix 4 used an un-asserted str.replace() whose guessed target string did not match, so it silently no-opped even though the script printed \"patched\") and how the agent diagnosed and targeted the repair (running grep -o \"deficiencies documented in writing .\\{0,20\\} earlier\" on /tmp/build_ps.py and grep -c \"nine months\" on /tmp/ps_out.txt, then deciding to patch directly against the verified current string rather than retry a guessed target). The report contains none of this: no mention of a silent failure, un-asserted replace, mismatched target string, the \"patched\" output, or the grep commands that located the surviving string. The report only gestures at the topic in two places — \"Found wrong month intervals and fixed them over several rounds; one leftover phrase took three attempts (cards 0077&ndash;0080)\" and \"It corrected &ldquo;five months&rdquo; and &ldquo;nine months&rdquo; after computing the December 18, 2023 to September 6, 2024 interval as about 8.6 months (cards 0067, 0068, 0074, 0080).\" The first notes the leftover phrase was hard to fix but gives no cause; the second explains why the wording was wrong (arithmetic), not why the earlier fix silently failed or how the agent targeted the repair. That is a vague topic mention that does not materially answer the question, so it is missing_from_report. (The report-only reader's answer likewise rests on these two passages and adds the same non-answer, framing the diagnosis as the arithmetic re-check rather than the grep-against-the-generator step shown in cards 0077-0079.)", "evidence": ["card_0075", "card_0076", "card_0077", "card_0078", "card_0079"]}, {"id": "question_33", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer (card_0080) is concrete: the final fix was applied by patching the builder script /tmp/build_ps.py with an asserted string replace (\"deficiencies documented in writing nine months earlier\" -> \"deficiencies documented in writing more than eight months earlier\", plus an assert that \"nine months\" no longer occurs), then re-running the builder (\"fixed IV\" / \"saved\"), re-rendering the docx via pandoc into /tmp/ps_out.txt, and confirming with grep counts — zero matches for \"nine months|five months\" and four matches for \"more than eight months\". The report contains none of this: no \"more than eight months\", no build_ps.py, no ps_out.txt, no grep counts, and no mention of Section IV. It only gestures at the topic — in the phase table: \"Found wrong month intervals and fixed them over several rounds; one leftover phrase took three attempts (cards 0077&ndash;0080)\" — and in the choices list: \"It corrected &ldquo;five months&rdquo; and &ldquo;nine months&rdquo; after computing the December 18, 2023 to September 6, 2024 interval as about 8.6 months (cards 0067, 0068, 0074, 0080)\". These say that a month-interval fix happened, not how the final Section IV fix was applied or which checks (the 0/4 grep counts) confirmed the text was finally clean, so a reader cannot learn the material answer. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT verdict is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0080"]}, {"id": "question_34", "answer": "After the last content edits (the month-interval corrections and the PIP-target sentence reword, phase 5), the agent moved to phase 6: it ran 14 structural checks (all passed), confirmed no \"Vanguard\" or \"Valiant\" text in the draft, appended a verification log to the notes, and wrote the closing message. It made no further content changes to the position-statement deliverable.", "quote": "5. Verification and repair 0061–0081 Opened the file with pandoc and python-docx, counted key strings, and re-did the arithmetic. Found wrong month intervals and fixed them over several rounds; one leftover phrase took three attempts (cards 0077–0080). Reworded a sentence about the PIP target that read as ambiguous. 6. Final checks and report 0082–0085 Ran 14 structural checks (all passed), confirmed no “Vanguard” or “Valiant” text in the draft, appended a verification log to the notes, and wrote the closing message.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report's phase table answers the question: the last content changes (month-interval fixes and the PIP-target reword) are placed in phase 5 (cards 0061&ndash;0081), and phase 6 (cards 0082&ndash;0085) contains only verification and closing actions &mdash; 14 structural checks all passed, a check that no Vanguard/Valiant text remained, a verification log appended to the notes, and the closing message &mdash; with no further edits to the .docx listed. The \"How the work was checked\" section reinforces that the final phase was verification-only (14 checks all passed, spot checks re-run, arithmetic recomputed and consistent). This matches what card_0081 (final fact-check pass), card_0082 (14-point programmatic structure check, ALL PASS), card_0083 (re-verification of facts/math/quotes), and card_0084 (Vanguard/Valiant and percentage greps on the pandoc text plus appending the \"FINAL VERIFICATION\" section to process/notes.md marking the deliverable complete) show. The report conveys the material answer &mdash; the post-edit work was verification-only and the content was settled &mdash; through the phase structure rather than a literal \"no further changes\" sentence, but the phase-6 row enumerates only non-editing actions, so the answer is carried by the report's own text.", "evidence": ["card_0081", "card_0082", "card_0083", "card_0084"]}, {"id": "question_35", "answer": "The agent's programmatic structure check consisted of 14 structural checks of the built position-statement document, covering the addressee, the charge number, each allegation, the confidential marking, the exhibit list, and the signature block. The outcome was that all 14 checks passed (card_0082). As part of the same final-check stage, the agent also confirmed there was no \"Vanguard\" or \"Valiant\" text in the draft, appended a verification log to its notes, and wrote the closing message (cards 0082-0085).", "quote": "Structure. 14 checks covering addressee, charge number, each allegation, the confidential marking, the exhibit list and the signature block all passed (card_0082).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report states that the programmatic structure check ran 14 checks covering the addressee, charge number, each allegation, the confidential marking, the exhibit list and the signature block, and that all passed, citing card_0082. That matches card_0082, whose python-docx script evaluates 14 named checks (addressee, Strickland attention line, charge number multiplicity, the four allegation headings, PIP/termination/severance sections, retaliation-elements section, confidential marking, CA-1..CA-5, Exhibits A–J, Milliken signature block, counsel, dismissal request) and prints ALL PASS, and card_0083, whose appended verification log records the same structural check as ALL PASS. The report summarizes the 14 checks by category rather than listing every exact string, but it conveys the material trace-grounded answer and outcome; the report-only reader's extraction is supported by the report text.", "evidence": ["card_0082", "card_0083"]}, {"id": "question_36", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report materially answers both halves of the question. On the name-leakage check, the \"What the agent did, in order\" table states the final checks \"confirmed no 'Vanguard' or 'Valiant' text in the draft,\" and the \"How the work was checked\" section explains the mechanism: strings were counted in the pandoc-extracted text (\"About 30 strings were counted in the extracted text\"), i.e., a programmatic string-count check on the extracted text found no conflicting names — matching card_0084's grep of the extracted text (count 0) and the Saxonbrook-throughout conclusion. On the arithmetic and PIP-target phrasing, the report says the \"Severance, percentage reductions and day intervals were recomputed and found consistent,\" and its draft-position table gives the very figures re-derived in cards 0081/0083/0084: 14.8 vs the 10-day standard, a 32% reduction achieved against the 45% reduction the corrected target required, with the 12-day target described as more lenient than the 10-day standard. It also records that the ambiguous PIP-target sentence was reworded, which is the correction the question asks about. The report omits the exact grep command, the literal count-0, and the line numbers (111/113/123), but the substance of the trace-grounded answer — how the absence was confirmed (string counts on the extracted text) and what was concluded (no leakage; arithmetic consistent; corrected PIP-target phrasing present) — is communicated and is supported by cards 0081, 0083, and 0084. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is a miss: its own \"unlocatable_evidence\" entries quote passages that are verbatim in the report (verified by search), so the report does answer the question.", "evidence": ["card_0081", "card_0083", "card_0084"]}, {"id": "question_37", "answer": "As its last check before writing the closing (user-facing) summary, the agent confirmed that no \"Vanguard\" or \"Valiant\" text remained in the draft. Its purpose was to keep the company name consistent with the charge and transmittal, which use \"Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.\", since the internal documents (review form, PIP, EEO policy) used the conflicting names Vanguard and Valiant.", "quote": "Ran 14 structural checks (all passed), confirmed no “Vanguard” or “Valiant” text in the draft, appended a verification log to the notes, and wrote the closing message.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "Per card_0085, the agent's last check before the final summary was to verify the deliverable's file location and that vpm-position-statement.docx is the only artifact at the requested path - the last guard on the single-output-file requirement. The report does not contain this. Its Phase 6 row (\"Ran 14 structural checks (all passed), confirmed no &ldquo;Vanguard&rdquo; or &ldquo;Valiant&rdquo; text in the draft, appended a verification log to the notes, and wrote the closing message.\") describes the final checks but lists no location/sole-artifact verification, and nowhere in the report do the words \"only artifact\", \"file location\", or \"expected path\" appear (verified by grep). The reader's extraction picked up the Vanguard/Valiant name check, which the report does contain, but that is a different check than the gold answer and the report never presents it as the last check before the summary. The closest the report gets are incidental mentions (\"The agent produced one Word file, <code>/app/workspace/vpm-position-statement.docx<\/code>\" and \"left the .docx in place\"), which state a fact about the output rather than describing a verification step the agent decided to run and its purpose. The material answer is therefore absent from the report.", "evidence": ["card_0085"]}, {"id": "question_38", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question has two parts: what the agent reported as complete and verified in its terminal step (card_0085), and what follow-ups it offered. The report only addresses the first part, and then through critique rather than as a summary of the terminal message: it says the closing message calls the work &ldquo;complete and verified&rdquo;, describes the deliverable (letter to the EEOC Atlanta District Office dated April 18, 2025 with a confidential CA section), the mechanical checks (261 extracted lines, ~30 spot checks, 14 structural checks, no Vanguard/Valiant text), and the error fixes (month-math slips, ambiguous PIP-target rewording). But it never communicates the second part of the question at all: the report contains no mention of the optional follow-ups the agent offered before finishing (export a PDF preview, or adjust tone/scope, e.g., expand the comparator section or add a privilege log for CA-3). A grep of the report for pdf/preview/follow-up/privilege/CA-3/expand/tone-scope offers finds nothing; the only \"closing message\" mentions are the report's own critiques (\"The closing message calls the work &ldquo;complete and verified.&rdquo; ...\", \"The closing message overstates coverage.\"). That is a topic gesture without answering what follow-ups were offered, so the conjunctive question is not materially answered by the report, matching the reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction on the follow-up half.", "evidence": ["card_0085"]}, {"id": "question_39", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks both how the .docx was produced and what rationale the agent gave for generating it with a python-docx script. The report only gestures at the \"how\": its phase table says of cards 0059-0060 \"Wrote one Python script containing the full text and ran it to produce the .docx,\" and separately notes python-docx was installed via pip and the file was later opened with pandoc/python-docx for checks. That conveys the basic mechanism but omits the script specifics (heredoc to /tmp/build_ps.py run in the same command, output \"saved\"). More importantly, the report never states the agent's stated rationale - that python-docx produces a standard docx that pandoc can open so the deliverable could be converted to plain text and verified - and the report's \"Why the agent made its main choices\" section (respondent name, disclosure, defense structure, employee ID, date arithmetic) contains no entry on the document-generation method or its pandoc-compatibility reasons (Times New Roman 12pt/1.15 spacing, tables preserved by pandoc, plain-paragraph Re: line, page break before CA-1, CONFIDENTIAL banner). A report-only reader cannot learn the rationale from the report, so the material answer is absent.", "evidence": ["card_0059", "card_0060"]}, {"id": "question_40", "answer": "The agent split the content by keeping the letter body disclosable and moving sensitive material into the confidential attachments CA-1 through CA-5. The principle driving the split was the confidentiality guidance in the EEOC transmittal letter: the disclosable body answers each allegation, while comparator data, witness accounts, and company-wide statistics (and only-confidential details such as Harmon's employee ID) sit in the confidential attachments marked not for disclosure to the charging party.", "quote": "It kept the letter body disclosable and moved sensitive material into a confidential attachment, following the transmittal’s guidance (cards 0015, 0059).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report communicates the material trace-grounded answer. It states the decision — the letter body was kept disclosable while sensitive material went into the confidential attachments CA-1 through CA-5, in a separate section marked not for disclosure to the charging party — and gives the driving principle: the confidentiality guidance in the EEOC transmittal letter. This matches the trace: card_0015 records the transmittal's rule (body of statement IS disclosed to charging party; sensitive personnel data -> separate CONFIDENTIAL attachment), and card_0059's content plan implements exactly that split, with the per-attachment assignments (comparator metrics CA-1, witness accounts CA-2, privileged investigation summary CA-3, company-wide termination/PIP statistics CA-4, records index CA-5) corresponding to the categories the report names (comparator data, witness accounts, company-wide statistics). The report omits two refinements from the gold answer — that the body may still restate the 3.1/2.8 scores because the charge itself is a public document, and the page-break/confidential-banner formatting detail — but the core decision and the principle behind the split are present, accurate, and carried by the report's own text. The report-only reader's extraction is consistent with the report and its quote was located verbatim.", "evidence": ["card_0059", "card_0015"]}], "stages": {"spend": {"census": {"sessions": 0, "seconds": 0}, "questions": {"sessions": 0, "seconds": 0}, "answer": {"sessions": 53, "seconds": 19960}, "judge": {"sessions": 39, "seconds": 24875}, "claims": {"sessions": 128, "seconds": 27468}, "recoveries": {"sessions": 4, "seconds": 794}}, "claims": [{"id": "c1", "claim": "The agent drafted a position statement for Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc. (\"VPM\") responding to Charge No. 410-2025-01847.", "type": "outcome_claim", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0060 shows the agent building /app/workspace/vpm-position-statement.docx with python-docx: a Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc. letterhead position statement dated April 18, 2025 to the EEOC Atlanta District Office (Attention: Investigator Paul D. Strickland), with \"Re: Charge No. 410-2025-01847\" and \"Respondent: Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc. (\"VPM\" or the \"Company\")\". card_0011 shows the underlying charge document confirms Charge No. 410-2025-01847. card_0082 verifies the generated file exists and passes all content checks (charge number present 3+ times, addressee, signature block, \"ALL PASS\"). This matches the claim exactly."}, {"id": "c2", "claim": "The agent produced one Word file, /app/workspace/vpm-position-statement.docx.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0060 shows the agent's python-docx build script ending with doc.save('/app/workspace/vpm-position-statement.docx') and printing \"saved\", creating exactly that file; card_0082 shows a later `ls -la` in /app/workspace listing only vpm-position-statement.docx (52,255 bytes) alongside the content checks; card_0085 (terminal summary) confirms the single deliverable is vpm-position-statement.docx. The other workspace .docx files were pre-existing inputs (card_0002), and the agent's only other written artifact was process/notes.md (markdown), so the claim of one produced Word file at that path holds."}, {"id": "c3", "claim": "The delivered file is a letter to the EEOC Atlanta District Office, dated April 18, 2025.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0064 renders the delivered vpm-position-statement.docx via pandoc and shows its header: letterhead for Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, dated \"April 18, 2025\", sent \"VIA EEOC Respondent Portal and Certified Mail\" to the \"U.S. Equal Employment Opportunity Commission, Atlanta District Office\", Re: Charge No. 410-2025-01847. card_0066 confirms this same docx was the final verified deliverable (261-line plain-text extraction spot-checked against source facts). Both the recipient (EEOC Atlanta District Office) and the date (April 18, 2025) match the claim exactly."}, {"id": "c4", "claim": "The letter answers each allegation in the charge and asks for dismissal.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0072 shows the extracted text of the delivered letter: a \"Response to Allegations\" section with subsections answering each allegation (A. FY2024 2.4 review; B. Northfield meeting exclusion; C. the \"keep up with the pace\" comment; D. Leadership Development non-nomination; E. PIP retaliation), ending with \"Based on the foregoing, VPM respectfully requests that the EEOC dismiss the Charge in its entirety.\" card_0085, the terminal summary, corroborates that the letter contains a response to every allegation (review, meeting, comment, LDP, PIP, termination, severance/backfill) followed by a conclusion requesting dismissal."}, {"id": "c5", "claim": "Comparator data, witness accounts and company-wide statistics sit in a separate section of the letter marked not for disclosure to the charging party.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0060 shows the document build script writing a page break followed by a centered \"CONFIDENTIAL / NOT FOR DISCLOSURE TO CHARGING PARTY\" section containing \"CONFIDENTIAL ATTACHMENT CA-1: COMPARATOR EMPLOYEE RECORDS\", \"CA-2: WITNESS ACCOUNTS\", and \"CA-4: COMPANY-WIDE PERFORMANCE-MANAGEMENT AND TERMINATION RECORDS\" — exactly the comparator data, witness accounts, and company-wide statistics the claim names, in a separate section marked not for disclosure to the charging party. card_0084's final verification of the built docx confirms the CONFIDENTIAL marking and CA-1..CA-5 attachments are present in the delivered letter."}, {"id": "c6", "claim": "The final state is a draft that was mechanically checked; the session evidence does not support a legal verification.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The trace's terminal state is a built draft (vpm-position-statement.docx) validated by programmatic checks only. card_0084 shows the final verification step: a grep for banned names, arithmetic re-checks, and a script-based structural PASS list (addressee, charge number, allegations, CA-1..CA-5, exhibits, signature block) all recorded in process/notes.md, plus fixes for 4 month-math errors found by those checks. card_0082 shows the same structural check printing \"PASS ... ALL PASS\". card_0085 (terminal card) summarizes \"Verification performed\" as a spot-check script on ~30 key facts and mechanical fixes, and describes the deliverable as a draft to be optionally expanded. Nowhere in the 85 ca … [311 more characters]"}, {"id": "c7", "claim": "The agent checked the file mechanically - text extraction, about 30 spot-checked names and figures, recalculated arithmetic, and 14 structural checks - and all of those checks passed.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0066/card_0070 show pandoc text extraction of vpm-position-statement.docx (132 paragraphs, valid OOXML). card_0085's final summary explicitly describes a \"spot-check script on ~30 key facts\" (ratings, NCR figures, $7,269.23, NAF-2024-NC-0042, termination stats, approval levels). card_0084 shows the arithmetic re-verified line by line ($7,269.23 = 94,500/52*4; 14.8 vs 10 = 48% above; 21.7→12 = 45% reduction; day-count and month-math). card_0082 shows exactly 14 structural checks executed programmatically (addressee, attention, charge number, 4 allegations, PIP, termination, severance/backfill, retaliation section, confidential marking, CA-1..5, exhibits A..J, signature block, counsel, di … [286 more characters]"}, {"id": "c8", "claim": "The agent fixed several errors it found along the way, mostly date intervals.", "type": "recovery", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The cards show multiple error fixes, most of them date-interval (month-math) corrections. card_0068 patches build_ps.py with four fixes: three month-math errors (\"five months\" and \"nine months\" -> \"more than eight months\" for the Dec 18, 2023 -> Sep 6, 2024 interval) plus one ambiguous PIP-target phrasing fix. card_0074 and card_0080 show further date fixes (\"fixed IV\", asserting \"nine months\" is gone; grep count of \"nine months|five months\" = 0, \"more than eight months\" = 4). card_0084 confirms the patched PIP-target phrasing is in the final extracted text and no name leakage. card_0085 (terminal) states the agent \"Fixed 5 errors found in the check: month-math slips (\"five/nine months\" -> \" … [137 more characters]"}, {"id": "c9", "claim": "The record shows no sentence-by-sentence comparison of the draft against the sources.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim is a negative about the record: no sentence-by-sentence comparison of the draft against the sources appears. The trajectory is detailed enough to confirm this. The verification steps that do appear are selective, not exhaustive: card_0081 shows the final fact-check pass as an enumerated list of ~30 key facts, dates, and specific quotes (e.g., ratings, NCR figures, severance math, exact-match quote checks) — a spot-check, not a sentence-by-sentence review; card_0084 shows the post-build verification as programmatic structure checks (addressee, sections, exhibits, naming) plus math re-verification; card_0085 (terminal summary) itself characterizes the check as a \"spot-check script on … [187 more characters]"}, {"id": "c10", "claim": "The record shows no legal review of the draft.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The full 85-card trajectory shows the agent drafting the position statement and self-verifying it (programmatic structure checks and fact spot-checks in card_0082 and card_0084; the agent declaring the deliverable complete in card_0085). The agent's plan (card_0004) contained only read/draft/verify steps with no legal-review step. No card records the draft being sent to, or reviewed by, counsel or any other party; \"counsel\" references (card_0053, card_0057) only name the firm of record, Blackwell, Thayer & Oakes LLP, that appears on the letterhead. So the record indeed shows no legal review of the draft."}, {"id": "c11", "claim": "The agent settled several conflicts in the source documents by its own choices and did not raise them with the user.", "type": "decision", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "Multiple source-document conflicts are visible in the trace, and each was settled by the agent's own unilateral choice: (1) company name — card_0039 shows the agent deciding \"For the formal entity name, I'll use the name listed in the EEOC charge/transmittal (Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.)\" despite letterheads varying across Saxonbrook/Vanguard/Valiant; card_0053 elaborates the same decision and also settles the employee-ID conflict (0247 vs 0284 vs 0847 vs VPM-1087) by choosing the review-compilation ID; (2) backfill statistics — card_0069 shows the two xlsx tabs disagreeing (12 vs 13 completed backfills with different race/sex breakdowns) and the agent deciding to cite the inter … [677 more characters]"}, {"id": "c12", "claim": "One figure the draft states as fact (13 completed backfills) conflicts with another tab in the same workbook.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim: the draft states \"13 completed backfills\" as a fact, and that figure conflicts with another tab in the same workbook. card_0081 confirms the draft text includes \"13 completed backfills, 7 white, 2 Black, 2 Hispanic, 2 Asian American; 8 male, 5 female.\" card_0044 shows vpm-termination-log.xlsx contains three tabs: Performance Terminations, Summary Statistics, and Replacement Hires. card_0046 (full dump) shows the Summary Statistics tab stating \"Positions Backfilled ... Total 13\" with race/sex breakdowns summing to 13. card_0069 (agent's reasoning during verification) shows the agent reading the Replacement Hires tab as stating \"Total Positions Backfilled (completed): 12\" with race … [343 more characters]"}, {"id": "c13", "claim": "The session's closing message called the work \"complete and verified.\"", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0085 is the terminal card (turn 88, roles include terminal) carrying the session's closing message to the user, which opens with \"Deliverable is complete and verified: `vpm-position-statement.docx` ...\". The claimed phrasing \"complete and verified\" appears verbatim in that closing message, so the claim is supported."}, {"id": "c14", "claim": "The Word file and the notes file the agent created are not in the report workspace, so their contents are described from the agent's own tool outputs.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "unverifiable", "why": "The claim has two parts. The second part is consistent with the cards: the agent did create /app/workspace/vpm-position-statement.docx (card_0060's build script; card_0082 lists it at 52255 bytes and shows pandoc/python-docx verification output) and process/notes.md (card_0015 writes it; card_0054/0058 append), and their contents do appear in the saved tool outputs, so describing them from tool outputs is plausible. But the load-bearing part of the claim is that the Word file and the notes file \"are not in [the report] workspace\" — a statement about the report author's environment, which the saved cards do not contain. The cards only record the agent's own filesystem (/app/workspace, where b … [483 more characters]"}, {"id": "c15", "claim": "The agent created a notes file in addition to the Word position statement.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0015 shows the agent creating the notes file with `cat > /app/workspace/process/notes.md` (a process-notes markdown for the VPM position statement) and the command echoing \"done\", so the notes file was written. card_0082 confirms the Word position statement (vpm-position-statement.docx, 52255 bytes) exists and passes all content checks, so the notes file was produced in addition to the Word document as the claim states."}, {"id": "c16", "claim": "The step cards do not include the original task prompt, so the agent's goal is inferred from its own notes and actions.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The saved cards begin at turn 4 (card_0001), whose evidence is only the agent's own reasoning (\"First, let's start by exploring the workspace...\"), and a grep over all 85 cards finds no user-role message or original task prompt anywhere in the trajectory. So the cards indeed do not include the task prompt. The goal is instead only recoverable from the agent's own artifacts: card_0004 shows its plan steps (\"Read charge + transmittal docs\", \"Read a...\"), card_0015 shows notes.md titled \"Process notes — VPM position statement (EEOC 410...)\", and card_0085's final summary describes the deliverable (EEOC position statement for charge 410-2025-01847). This matches the claim that the goal is inferr … [42 more characters]"}, {"id": "c17", "claim": "The workspace held 11 source files: seven Word documents, two emails and two spreadsheets.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0002 shows `find /app/workspace -type f | head -100` listing exactly 11 files: seven .docx Word documents (eeoc-charge-410-2025-01847, eeoc-transmittal-letter, poole-termination-memo, vpm-eeo-policy, harmon-pip-october-2024, harmon-performance-reviews, hr-investigation-summary), two .eml emails (harmon-complaint-email-sept-2024, poole-qa-deficiency-email-dec-2023), and two .xlsx spreadsheets (pip-tracking-log, vpm-termination-log). This matches the claim's count and type breakdown exactly."}, {"id": "c18", "claim": "The agent opened all 11 source files, though not all in a single read.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0002 lists the 11 source files in /app/workspace (7 docx, 2 eml, 2 xlsx). The trace shows each one opened, in separate reads: card_0011 pandocs eeoc-charge-410-2025-01847.docx and eeoc-transmittal-letter.docx; card_0016 pandocs harmon-performance-reviews, harmon-pip-october-2024, and hr-investigation-summary; card_0018 converts all 7 docx files to text (including vpm-eeo-policy and poole-termination-memo, confirmed by byte counts), with poole-termination-memo.txt read in card_0038 and vpm-eeo-policy.txt in card_0052; card_0040 cats both .eml files; card_0048 loads pip-tracking-log.xlsx and card_0046 loads vpm-termination-log.xlsx via openpyxl. All 11 files were opened, across many separ … [65 more characters]"}, {"id": "c19", "claim": "The task was completed in the final phase: the agent ran 14 structural checks (all passed), confirmed no \"Vanguard\" or \"Valiant\" text in the draft, appended a verification log to the notes, and wrote the closing message.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0082 shows the 14-key structural check dict (addressee, Strickland, charge number, 4 internal allegations, PIP, termination, severance/backfill, retaliation elements, confidential marking, CA-1..CA-5, exhibits A..J, Milliken signature, counsel, dismissal request) printed with every line \"PASS\" and \"ALL PASS\". card_0083 shows the agent deciding to also verify no Vanguard/Valiant references; card_0084 shows that grep -ic \"vanguard\\|valiant\" over the extracted draft text returning 0, and the same command appending the \"FINAL VERIFICATION\" log to process/notes.md (output ends \"done\"). card_0085 (terminal) is the closing message to the user summarizing the deliverable and verification. All f … [114 more characters]"}, {"id": "c20", "claim": "The work moved through six sequential phases: 1. Orientation, 2. Reading the sources, 3. Fact notes, 4. Drafting, 5. Verification and repair, and 6. Final checks and report.", "type": "task_transition", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The cards show the six phases in the claimed order. (1) Orientation: cards 0001-0010 list the workspace, find pandoc but not python-docx/openpyxl, and card_0004 shows the update_plan call failing as \"unsupported call: update_plan\"; card_0009 shows both pip-installed. (2) Reading sources: cards 0011-0056 convert the Word files with pandoc and read the xlsx files with openpyxl. (3) Fact notes: card_0015 creates process/notes.md, and cards 0054 and 0058 append supporting-doc facts and a cross-checked verification of key figures. (4) Drafting: card_0060 writes /tmp/build_ps.py with the full letter text and runs it to produce vpm-position-statement.docx. (5) Verification and repair: cards 0061-00 … [362 more characters]"}, {"id": "c21", "claim": "During orientation the agent found pandoc installed in the workspace, but python-docx and openpyxl missing.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0005 (turn 8, early orientation) shows the agent running `which pandoc python3`, which returned /usr/bin/pandoc (pandoc installed), and `python3 -c \"import docx, openpyxl\"` failing with a traceback, i.e. python-docx missing. card_0007 (turn 10) re-checks: `pip list` grep for docx/openpyxl returns nothing, and separate `import openpyxl` and `import docx` probes both traceback, confirming openpyxl and python-docx were missing before the agent pip-installed them (card_0009). This matches the claim that during orientation pandoc was found installed but python-docx and openpyxl were missing."}, {"id": "c22", "claim": "The agent installed python-docx and openpyxl with pip.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0009 shows the agent running `pip install python-docx openpyxl 2>&1 | tail -1`, which exited with code 0 (pip's upgrade notice is the last line, consistent with a successful install). card_0005 and card_0007 show both imports (docx, openpyxl) failed before the install, and card_0010 shows the agent proceeding to use pandoc/openpyxl afterwards, so the install was the enabling step the report describes."}, {"id": "c23", "claim": "An early plan-update call failed as an unsupported tool (card_0004).", "type": "runtime_failure", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0004 (turn 7, early in the 88-turn trajectory) records an update_plan tool call whose evidence reads \"unsupported call: update_plan\", i.e. the plan-update call failed as an unsupported tool, exactly as claimed."}, {"id": "c24", "claim": "The agent converted each Word source file to text with pandoc.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0018 shows the agent running `for f in *.docx; do pandoc \"$f\" -t plain --wrap=none -o /tmp/src/${f%.docx}.txt; done` in /app/workspace, with `wc -c` confirming text output for all seven .docx sources (eeoc-charge, eeoc-transmittal-letter, harmon-performance-reviews, harmon-pip-october-2024, hr-investigation-summary, poole-termination-memo, vpm-eeo-policy). The remaining non-.docx sources (.eml, .xlsx) are not Word files, so \"each Word source file\" is covered. Earlier cards (card_0011, card_0013, card_0016) corroborate pandoc being used to extract plain text from the .docx files."}, {"id": "c25", "claim": "The agent read the spreadsheets with openpyxl.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0046 shows the agent running python3 with `import openpyxl; openpyxl.load_workbook(\"vpm-termination-log.xlsx\", data_only=True)` and reading the \"Performance Terminations\" and \"Summary Statistics\" sheets; card_0048 shows openpyxl dumping sheets of pip-tracking-log.xlsx (NCR Closure Detail); card_0050 shows openpyxl.load_workbook(\"pip-tracking-log.xlsx\") reading the \"Corrective Action Status\" and \"Customer Audit Log\" sheets. The spreadsheets were read with openpyxl as claimed."}, {"id": "c26", "claim": "Several tool outputs while reading the sources were cut off by the tool.", "type": "runtime_failure", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "Multiple source-reading tool outputs were truncated by the tool. card_0011 (cat of the EEOC charge/transmittal docs) shows \"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 3532)\"; card_0016 (pandoc dump of the harmon-performance-reviews, harmon-pip-october-2024, and hr-investigation-summary sources) shows \"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 22951)\" with the evidence itself cut off at 800 of 10176 chars; card_0013 (pandoc of the EEOC charge to /tmp/charge.txt) shows \"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 7020)\"; and card_0020 (sed read of the performance-reviews text) shows \"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 2631)\". The agent's own notes (card_0012, ca … [90 more characters]"}, {"id": "c27", "claim": "To work around the cut-off outputs, the agent re-read line ranges and searched with grep.", "type": "recovery", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The cards show both the trigger and the recovery. card_0011 and card_0016 carry \"Warning: truncated output\" results, and the agent's reasoning explicitly acknowledges the cut-off output: card_0012 (\"The charging document was truncated partway through... read the truncated portion... read it in chunks\") and card_0017 (\"The output is truncated partway through... dump each document to a text file\"). In response the agent re-read line ranges: card_0013 (sed -n '1,400p' | sed -n '60,200p' on /tmp/charge.txt), card_0020 (sed -n '1,150p'), card_0024 (sed -n '253,336p'), card_0026 (sed -n '337,475p'), card_0032/0034 (sed ranges on hr-investigation-summary.txt), and card_0029 (\"Let's get the truncate … [337 more characters]"}, {"id": "c28", "claim": "The agent noted source conflicts as it went while reading the sources.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The cards show the agent flagging source conflicts at the moment it read the affected sources, not only in a final pass. card_0029, right after reading the PIP, notes the employee-ID discrepancy (VPM-2019-0284 vs 0247) \"should be recorded\"; card_0039 notes the ID and letterhead brand inconsistencies (Saxonbrook/Vanguard/Valiant) while deciding which sources remain to read; card_0047 flags the Sellers coaching-plan start-date discrepancy (11/01/2024 vs July 2024) while examining the termination log. card_0053 then consolidates the company-name and employee-ID inconsistencies found \"as I [read] the sources,\" and card_0054 appends a \"SOURCE INCONSISTENCIES (distractors)\" section with those conf … [103 more characters]"}, {"id": "c29", "claim": "The agent wrote process/notes.md containing key facts, figures, a defense map for each allegation, and a list of source conflicts with the choice made for each.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0015 shows process/notes.md written with key facts (transmittal facts, charge particulars, documents list). card_0054 appends verified supporting-doc facts and figures plus a \"SOURCE INCONSISTENCIES (distractors)\" section listing three conflicts (company name, employee ID, Sellers coaching-plan date) each with an explicit \"DECISION\" made. card_0058 appends \"Verification of key figures (cross-checked)\" (figures) and a \"Defense map (allegation -> legitimate reason)\" with a numbered defense for each of the 7 alleged items. All four claimed components appear in the file as written."}, {"id": "c30", "claim": "The agent planned a letter with numbered sections, lettered exhibits, and confidential attachments CA-1 to CA-5.", "type": "decision", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0054 records the agent's explicit \"Structure decision\" in its process notes: PART I = disclosable letter body with letterhead, date, addressee, Re line, \"I-VII sections\" (numbered sections) and a disclosable Exhibit list (A-I) (lettered exhibits), plus PART II = \"CONFIDENTIAL ATTACHMENT - NOT FOR DISCLOSURE TO CHARGING PARTY\" comprising CA-1 comparator records, CA-2 witness accounts, CA-3 investigation findings, CA-4 company-wide stats, and CA-5 records index. card_0060 shows the build script (build_ps.py) implementing exactly that plan: sections I., II., III. etc., Exhibits A-H, and headings CA-1 through CA-5 under the CONFIDENTIAL ATTACHMENT section, confirming the plan named in the c … [5 more characters]"}, {"id": "c31", "claim": "The agent wrote one Python script containing the full text and ran it to produce the .docx.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0060 shows the agent writing a single Python script via a heredoc to /tmp/build_ps.py containing the full position-statement text (styles, letterhead, all sections, exhibits, CA-1 through CA-5), ending with doc.save('/app/workspace/vpm-position-statement.docx'), and immediately running \"python3 /tmp/build_ps.py\", which printed \"saved\". card_0062 and card_0070 subsequently confirm the generated .docx file exists and opens/converts correctly. Later cards only patch the same single script, so the .docx was produced as claimed."}, {"id": "c32", "claim": "During verification the agent opened the file with pandoc and python-docx, counted key strings, and re-did the arithmetic.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The post-build verification phase shows all three claimed actions. (1) Opened the file with pandoc: card_0062 and card_0064 run `pandoc vpm-position-statement.docx -t plain` on the deliverable, and card_0066 extracts the full plain text to /tmp/ps_out.txt (261 lines). (2) Opened it with python-docx: card_0070 and card_0082 load the .docx with `from docx import Document` to run a programmatic structure check (card_0082 output ends \"ALL PASS\"). (3) Counted key strings: card_0066 loops over ~28 key strings (charge number, dates, names, figures) printing grep -c counts, and card_0084 greps \"vanguard|valiant\" and the percentage strings. (4) Re-did the arithmetic: card_0058 records \"Verification o … [221 more characters]"}, {"id": "c33", "claim": "Verification found wrong month intervals in the draft.", "type": "runtime_failure", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0067 shows the agent re-reading its own draft (vpm-position-statement.docx) and flagging wrong month intervals: \"more than five months\" understating a 8.5-month gap, and \"nine months earlier/before the complaint\" overstating 8.6-8.8 months, deciding to correct both to \"more than eight months.\" card_0068 shows the applied patch (Fixes 1-4) replacing the incorrect month phrasings and re-generating the doc, and card_0074 shows a further wrong-interval fix (\"five months after the underlying deficiencies\" → \"more than eight months after\"). The verification pass therefore did find wrong month intervals in the draft, as the claim states."}, {"id": "c34", "claim": "The agent fixed the wrong month intervals over several rounds.", "type": "recovery", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The trace shows the agent discovering and fixing month-math errors (\"five months\" / \"nine months\" where the correct interval was \"more than eight months\", Dec 18 2023 -> Sep 6 2024) in distinct rounds: card_0068 (turn 71) applies a batch of month-math patches (Fixes 1, 3, 4) to build_ps.py; card_0074 (turn 77) fixes a remaining \"five months\" instance in section III.A.4 that survived the first patch; card_0080 (turn 83) fixes a remaining \"nine months earlier\" instance in section IV and verifies zero occurrences of the wrong phrasing remain. card_0084 (turn 84) records in process/notes.md that \"4 month-math errors (5/9 months -> 'more than eight months')\" were \"fixed during verification\", conf … [66 more characters]"}, {"id": "c35", "claim": "One leftover phrase took three attempts to fix (cards 0077&ndash;0080).", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The leftover phrase is the month-math wording \"deficiencies documented in writing nine months earlier\" in section IV. card_0068 (turn 71) shows attempt 1: a four-fix patch whose Fix 4 replaced exactly that section-IV string without asserting it, and its grep output showed the old \"nine months earlier\" phrasing still present in section IV — a silent miss. card_0077 (turn 80) shows the agent noticing that \"nine months earlier\" still remains in section IV despite the earlier patch. card_0078 (turn 81) greps build_ps.py and confirms \"deficiencies documented in writing nine months earlier\" is still there with 1 \"nine months\" left in the document. card_0079 (turn 82) diagnoses why the first replac … [354 more characters]"}, {"id": "c36", "claim": "The agent reworded a sentence about the PIP target that read as ambiguous.", "type": "recovery", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0067 shows the agent flagging the exact sentence: the Section III.E.2 PIP-target clause \"an interim target deliberately set below the Company's 10-business-day standard…\" where \"below\" reads as ambiguous (12 days is numerically above 10, i.e. more lenient), deciding \"Let me fix this wording for accuracy.\" card_0068 shows the follow-up patch with \"Fix 2: ambiguous PIP target phrasing\", replacing that clause with \"deliberately set at a level more lenient than the Company's 10-business-day standard in light of the 21.7-day baseline\", and rebuilding/extracting the document. card_0084's final verification notes record the fix: \"ambiguous PIP-target phrasing (now: 'interim target deliberately … [147 more characters]"}, {"id": "c37", "claim": "The agent ran 14 structural checks on the draft, and all of them passed.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0082 is the agent's programmatic structure-check of the draft docx: its output lists exactly 14 PASS lines (addressee EEOC Atlanta, attention Strickland, charge number, all 4 internal allegations addressed, PIP addressed, termination addressed, severance/backfill addressed, retaliation elements section, confidential marking, CA-1..CA-5 present, exhibits A..J, signature block Milliken, counsel, request dismissal) followed by \"ALL PASS\", matching the claim of 14 structural checks all passing. card_0084 records the same 14-item structure check in process/notes.md as \"ALL PASS\", and card_0085 confirms \"All checks pass\"."}, {"id": "c38", "claim": "The agent confirmed that no \"Vanguard\" or \"Valiant\" text appears in the draft.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0084 shows the agent grepping the extracted draft text (/tmp/ps_out.txt) with `grep -ic \"vanguard\\|valiant\"` and getting a count of 0, and records \"No Vanguard/Valiant name leakage (grep count = 0)\" in the final verification notes. card_0085's terminal summary confirms the agent reported this check as passing. The claim that the agent confirmed no \"Vanguard\" or \"Valiant\" text in the draft matches the trace."}, {"id": "c39", "claim": "The agent appended a verification log to the notes.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0054 shows the agent appending (cat >>) a \"## Supporting-doc facts (verified against sources)\" section to /app/workspace/process/notes.md; card_0058 shows a second cat >> append of a \"## Verification of key figures (cross-checked)\" section to the same notes file, completing successfully (output \"ok\"). Together these are a verification log appended to the notes, as claimed."}, {"id": "c40", "claim": "The agent wrote the closing message.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0085 is the terminal turn of the trace (turn 88, roles include decision/terminal) and carries the agent's final assistant message — a closing summary of the deliverable (\"Deliverable is complete and verified: vpm-position-statement.docx ...\") ending with an offer to export a PDF. The trace ends with that message, so the agent did write the closing message."}, {"id": "c41", "claim": "The agent used \"Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.\" as the respondent name because the charge and the EEOC transmittal use that name, while several internal documents use other names (cards 0039, 0054).", "type": "decision", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0039 records the agent's decision at turn 42: noting \"brand inconsistencies in the document letterheads (Saxonbrook, Vanguard, Valiant)\", it says \"For the formal entity name, I'll use the name listed in the EEOC complaint/cover letter (Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.)\" — exactly the claimed decision and reason. card_0054 (turn 57) writes this into the process notes as an explicit \"SOURCE INCONSISTENCIES (distractors)\" entry: charge + transmittal = \"Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.\" (authoritative), while internal docs vary (\"Vanguard Precision Manufacturing\" in reviews, PIP, EEO policy, email domains; \"VALIANT PRECISION MANUFACTURING\" in the termination memo), with \"DEC … [598 more characters]"}, {"id": "c42", "claim": "The agent kept the letter body disclosable and moved sensitive material into a confidential attachment, following the transmittal's guidance (cards 0015, 0059).", "type": "decision", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0015 records the transmittal's guidance in the agent's process notes (\"Body of statement IS disclosed to charging party; sensitive personnel data -> separate CONFIDENTIAL attachment\"). card_0059 shows the agent's planning decision to implement exactly that: keep the body disclosable (general references plus Exhibits A-J) and move sensitive material (named comparator details, witness statements, investigation findings, NCR data) into a clearly marked Confidential Attachment. The final artifact confirms execution: card_0082 shows the structure check passing for the CONFIDENTIAL marking and CA-1..CA-5, and card_0085 states the sensitive sections sit in a page-broken section marked \"CONFIDE … [92 more characters]"}, {"id": "c43", "claim": "For the defense structure, the agent tied each allegation to a documented reason.", "type": "decision", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0060 shows the position statement being built with a Section III \"RESPONSE TO THE ALLEGATIONS\" whose seven subsections (A. September 6, 2024 performance review; B. Northfield meeting exclusion; C. August 22, 2024 comment; D. LDP non-nomination; E. October 14, 2024 PIP; F. January 17, 2025 termination; G. severance/backfill) each correspond to one allegation and refute it on documented grounds (objective quality metrics, the PIP and tracking records, the three-stage termination approval, investigation findings). The introduction states \"each specific allegation in the Charge is refuted by the documentary record,\" and every adverse action is tied to documented performance records. card_00 … [292 more characters]"}, {"id": "c44", "claim": "The agent anchored the argument on the supervisor's December 18, 2023 email, which predates the September 12, 2024 complaint (card_0058).", "type": "decision", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0058 shows the agent's defense map anchored the anti-retaliation argument on the pre-complaint documentation: \"FY2024 review 9/6/24 = 6 days BEFORE 9/12/24 complaint. Pre-complaint def doc 12/18/23 (23.4bd)\" and \"PIP retaliation -> predicate review predates complaint by 6d; deficiencies documented 12/18/23\", with the timeline \"review 9/6/24 -> complaint 9/12/24\". card_0054 attributes the 12/18/23 document to the supervisor (\"Poole email 12/18/23 to Milliken ... (predates complaint)\"), matching the \"supervisor's email\" framing. card_0085 confirms the final deliverable carried this anchor into the position statement's Factual Background (\"pre-complaint Dec 18, 2023 documentation\") and ret … [180 more characters]"}, {"id": "c45", "claim": "The agent used the employee ID from the formal review compilation prepared for counsel.", "type": "decision", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0016 shows the formal performance review compilation's header listing \"Employee ID: VPM-2019-0247\", and card_0022 shows that same compilation's note stating it \"was prepared at the request of counsel — Blackwell, Thayer & Oakes LLP\". card_0053 records the agent's explicit decision, after noticing the four conflicting IDs (0247/0284/0847/1087), to use VPM-2019-0247 because it is \"in the header of the most formal personnel-file compilation prepared at counsel's request\". card_0060 shows the position-statement build script embedding \"Denise L. Harmon personnel file (VPM-2019-0247)\" into the deliverable, and card_0071 re-confirms the ID was used. The agent used the employee ID from the form … [63 more characters]"}, {"id": "c46", "claim": "The agent placed the employee ID only in the confidential attachment (cards 0054, 0060).", "type": "decision", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0054 records the agent's decision on the conflicting employee IDs (reviews=VPM-2019-0247, PIP=VPM-2019-0284, tracking=VPM-2019-0847, term log=VPM-1087): use VPM-2019-0247 from the formal review compilation and minimize its use. card_0060 shows the position-statement build script in which VPM-2019-0247 appears exactly once, inside the \"CONFIDENTIAL ATTACHMENT CA-5: RECORDS MAINTAINED BY VPM IN THIS MATTER\" block (\"Denise L. Harmon personnel file (VPM-2019-0247)\"), and nowhere in the main body or other attachments. card_0071 confirms the agent's own re-check that \"VPM-2019-0247\" is used only in CA-5. So the employee ID was indeed placed only in the confidential attachment, as the claim st … [5 more characters]"}, {"id": "c47", "claim": "The agent corrected \"five months\" and \"nine months\" after computing the December 18, 2023 to September 6, 2024 interval as about 8.6 months (cards 0067, 0068, 0074, 0080).", "type": "decision", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0067 shows the agent's reasoning computing the December 18, 2023 to September 6, 2024 interval as 8.6 months and deciding that both \"more than five months\" (Section II.E) and \"nine months earlier\" (Section IV) were imprecise, to be corrected to \"more than eight months.\" card_0068 shows a patch applied to build_ps.py (output \"patched / saved\") and a grep of the rendered docx confirming \"more than eight months after Poole first documented the underlying deficiencies in writing\" on line 71. card_0074 shows the \"five months after the underlying deficiencies were first documented in writing\" string replaced with \"more than eight months\" (output \"fixed III.A.4\"). card_0080 shows the final \"de … [341 more characters]"}, {"id": "c48", "claim": "The draft takes the company's position on each allegation in the charge.", "type": "decision", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0015 lists the charge's alleged events (the 9/6/24 2.4 rating, Northfield meeting exclusion, 8/22/24 \"pace\" comment, LDP non-nomination, 10/14/24 PIP, 1/17/25 termination, severance offer) as the allegations to be answered. card_0067 shows the draft's Section III responses addressing each of those allegations individually from the company's (VPM's) position — e.g., III.A.3 using the comparators' ratings recited in the Charge, III.B the meeting agenda as Exhibit E, III.F.2 the three-stage approval in response to the sham-investigation allegation, III.E the PIP targets. card_0085's terminal summary confirms the delivered statement contains \"Response to every allegation (2.4 review; Northf … [189 more characters]"}, {"id": "c49", "claim": "For the FY2024 rating of 2.4 (Sept 6, 2024), the draft argues that it reflects documented metrics: an NCR closure average of 21.7 business days against a 10-day standard, the most customer-audit findings of the three managers, and two overdue corrective actions, and that the deficiencies were written up on Dec 18, 2023, before any complaint.", "type": "decision", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0072 shows the rendered draft's response to the FY2024 2.4 rating arguing the ratings \"track the documented differences in each employee's performance data,\" verified by the investigation, with supporting metrics in CA-1; card_0081's final fact-check confirms the CA-1 passage in the draft states \"Harmon's NCR closure time average of 21.7 business days was the highest among the three managers, and her group was responsible for the most customer audit findings during the review period. Harmon also had two overdue corrective action items\" (marked exact match), matching the claim's 21.7-vs-10-day-standard, most-audit-findings, and two-overdue-CAR metrics. card_0076 shows the draft's Section … [330 more characters]"}, {"id": "c50", "claim": "The agent recorded the basis for the FY2024 rating position as the investigation summary (card_0034) and notes (card_0054, card_0058), with sub-scores 3.0, 2.0, 2.5, 2.5, 2.0 averaging to 2.4.", "type": "other", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0054 shows the agent appending process notes recording the ratings basis: \"FY2024 (Poole, ...) 2.4. FY2024 sub-scores: Tech 3.0, Leadership 2.0, Process 2.5, Comm 2.5, Initiative 2.0\" with \"5 sub-cats avg to composite (no rounding)\" — exactly the sub-scores in the claim, which average to 2.4. card_0034 shows the investigation summary stating the \"overall rating of 2.4 was consistent with the documented performance metrics,\" which is the basis the claim attributes to it. card_0058's verification/defense-map section records the \"2.4 review -> objective documented metrics\" basis, confirming the recorded grounding."}, {"id": "c51", "claim": "For the Northfield meeting exclusion (Sept 3, 2024), the draft argues that the meeting agenda was NDT-specific, not QA.", "type": "decision", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0059 shows the drafted position statement's Section III.B (\"Exclusion from the Northfield meeting (September 3, 2024)\") arguing exactly what the claim states: \"The agenda was NDT-specific: NDT Level III personnel qualification review; Nadcap audit preparation timeline/readiness; Northfield NDT specification flow-down. No QA documentation / FAI / in-process audit topics.\" card_0053 and card_0058 show the same argument recorded in the agent's process notes as the chosen defense for the Northfield exclusion (\"NDT-only agenda ... no QA topics\"), and card_0015 confirms the underlying 9/3/24 Northfield exclusion allegation the draft responds to."}, {"id": "c52", "claim": "The agent recorded the basis for the Northfield meeting position as the agent's notes only (card_0058).", "type": "other", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0058 (turn 61) is exactly the event the claim describes: the agent appended to /app/workspace/process/notes.md (the agent's own notes file) a \"Defense map (allegation -> legitimate reason)\" section whose item 2 records the basis for the Northfield meeting position — \"Northfield exclusion -> NDT-only agenda (Nadcap/NDT Level III/spec flow-down); no QA topics\". This is the only card that writes that defense basis into the agent's notes: the earlier notes writes do not contain it (card_0015 creates notes.md with only the task/plan; card_0054's append covers verified facts, source inconsistencies, and the deliverable outline — \"B Northfield meeting\" appears there only as a structural placeh … [162 more characters]"}, {"id": "c53", "claim": "For the \"Keep up with the pace\" remark (Aug 22, 2024), the draft argues that it was a general remark about backlogs and audit deadlines, and that two witnesses did not corroborate it as aimed at Harmon.", "type": "decision", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0060 shows the actual draft (build_ps.py for vpm-position-statement.docx) arguing, for the August 22, 2024 \"keep up with the pace\" remark, that Poole \"denied directing the comment at Ms. Harmon and stated that it was a general statement about departmental performance expectations, made in the context of discussing backlogged NCRs and upcoming audit deadlines,\" and that \"two technicians who were present at the meeting were interviewed,\" each recalling only a general comment and not recalling Poole looking at Harmon specifically — i.e., two witnesses did not corroborate it was aimed at Harmon. card_0057's notes record the same point explicitly (\"2 witnesses (Goss, Whitford) did not corrob … [156 more characters]"}, {"id": "c54", "claim": "The agent recorded the basis for the remark position as the agent's notes only (card_0058).", "type": "other", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0058 shows the agent appending a \"Defense map\" section to its process notes\n(/app/workspace/process/notes.md) in which item 3 records the basis for the\n\"pace\" remark position: general departmental statement re NCR backlogs/audit\ndeadlines, 2 witnesses did NOT corroborate it was directed at Harmon, no\nprotected-class words. That notes entry is the recorded basis for the remark\nposition, and card_0054 (the same notes file) is the only other place the\nagent kept it — the trace shows no other dedicated record of the remark's\nbasis. The report's passage \"Agent's notes only (card_0058)\" matches what the\ntrace shows."}, {"id": "c55", "claim": "For the Leadership Development Program non-nomination, the draft argues that the program requires 18 months under the current supervisor, that Harmon had about 11 months, and that neither comparator was nominated either.", "type": "decision", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0060 (the build script that generates the position statement draft) shows the LDP non-nomination argument exactly as claimed: Harmon \"did not meet the program's facially neutral eighteen-month tenure requirement\" (~eleven months under Poole as of the September 1, 2024 nomination deadline), and \"neither Mr. Yamashiro nor Mr. Sellers ... was nominated in that cycle,\" both having the same tenure position. card_0053 shows the draft's underlying facts from the HR investigation summary: 18-month continuous-tenure requirement under the current supervisor (Section 3.2), Harmon under Poole since October 2, 2023 = ~11 months, and neither comparator nominated. card_0069 is the agent's own cross-ch … [119 more characters]"}, {"id": "c56", "claim": "The agent recorded the basis for the non-nomination position as a quoted program guideline (card_0081) and a tenure figure from the investigation summary (card_0081).", "type": "other", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0081 (the agent's final fact-check pass on the rendered position statement) shows the non-nomination basis recorded with both elements the claim names: item 34 verifies the quoted LDP program guideline, \"Nominees must have a minimum of eighteen (18) months of continuous tenure under their current direct supervisor at the time of the nomination deadline\" (Section 3.2) as an exact match, and item 26 verifies the tenure figure \"approximately 11 months\" as of the Sept 1, 2024 nomination deadline, expressly attributing it to the investigation summary (\"the investigation states 'approximately 11 months'\"). card_0060 shows this basis carried into the delivered docx: the III.E/CA-3 text states … [263 more characters]"}, {"id": "c57", "claim": "For the PIP (Oct 14, 2024), the draft argues that targets were measurable and communicated in advance, that the 12-day NCR target was more lenient than the 10-day standard, and that Harmon reached 14.8 days, a 32% reduction, against the 45% reduction the target required.", "type": "decision", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0084 shows the final draft text as built and verified: line 111 states \"The PIP targets were measurable, communicated in advance, and in a material respect more lenient than Company standards\" and describes the 12-business-day NCR target as \"deliberately set at a level more lenient than the Company's 10-business-day standard... requiring a 45% reduction over 90 days,\" and line 113 states Harmon's average NCR closure time improved \"from 21.7 to 14.8 business days — a 32% reduction.\" card_0057 shows the same figures cross-verified against the source data (NCR 21.7 → 14.8 = 32% reduction; 21.7 → 12 = 45% reduction required; 12 bd target more lenient than 10 bd standard). card_0053 confirms … [179 more characters]"}, {"id": "c58", "claim": "The agent recorded the basis for the PIP position as PIP tracking figures (card_0048; card_0054) and arithmetic (card_0058).", "type": "other", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0048 shows the agent loading pip-tracking-log.xlsx (NCR Closure Detail, Corrective Action Status, etc.) with openpyxl, the source of the PIP tracking figures. card_0054 shows the agent appending to process/notes.md the recorded PIP position basis: PIP objectives and results (NCR 21.7 -> 14.8 bd, 4/6 CARs on time, 1 customer NC, target 12 bd vs 10 bd std, 32% reduction) and a defense map keying the PIP-retaliation position to those objective metrics. card_0058 shows the agent appending a \"Verification of key figures (cross-checked)\" section with explicit arithmetic (e.g., 94,500/52*4 = 7,269.23; 45% reduction required vs 52.7% for full std; 32% reduction achieved) alongside the same PIP- … [140 more characters]"}, {"id": "c59", "claim": "For the termination (Jan 17, 2025), the draft argues that Harmon missed three of four PIP objectives and that termination required three levels of approval: supervisor recommendation, HR concurrence, and CEO approval.", "type": "decision", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0060 (the build script for the draft position statement) shows section \"F. The January 17, 2025 Termination Was a Legitimate, Performance-Based Decision\" stating Objective 1 (NCR closure), Objective 2 (corrective actions), and Objective 3 (zero customer audit NCs) were \"not met\" while Objective 4 was only \"not fully met\" (11 of 13, excused/holiday) — i.e., the draft argues Harmon missed three of four PIP objectives. The same section describes the termination decision as a \"deliberate, multi-level approval process\": Poole's written recommendation Jan 13, 2025; HR VP Milliken's concurrence Jan 15, 2025; CEO Tatum's approval Jan 16, 2025, effective Jan 17, 2025, and calls it a \"three-level … [337 more characters]"}, {"id": "c60", "claim": "The agent recorded the basis for the termination position as the termination log outcome (card_0046) and approval dates (card_0054).", "type": "other", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0046 shows the agent reading vpm-termination-log.xlsx and obtaining the termination outcome for Harmon (PIP Outcome: Failed — Did not meet 3 of 4 objectives; Termination Date 01/17/2025; Termination Reason: Failure to meet PIP objectives). card_0054 shows the agent appending those facts to process/notes.md as the termination position basis, including the termination-log pattern data and the approval dates (Poole rec 1/13/25, Milliken concur 1/15/25, CEO Tatum approved 1/16/25, eff 1/17/25), matching the report passage that cites card_0046 and card_0054 as the basis."}, {"id": "c61", "claim": "For severance and backfill, the draft argues that four weeks of pay, $7,269.23 ($94,500 divided by 52, times 4), was offered on condition of a release and was declined, and that a replacement was hired March 10, 2025 at $97,000.", "type": "decision", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0060 (the build script that writes the draft) contains the exact argument: Section III.G \"The Post-Termination Severance Offer and Position Backfill\" states VPM's offer of \"four weeks of severance pay ($7,269.23), conditioned on a release of claims, which Ms. Harmon declined,\" and that \"VPM filled the vacancy on March 10, 2025\"; CA-4 adds the replacement was filled March 10, 2025 \"with a starting salary of $97,000.\" card_0059 (agent notes for that section) matches: 4 weeks = $7,269.23 conditioned on a release, Harmon declined; replacement David A. Ruiz hired March 10, 2025 at $97,000. card_0066 spot-checks the built docx and finds 7,269.23 (1), 97,000 (1), March 10, 2025 (2), and 94,500 … [340 more characters]"}, {"id": "c62", "claim": "The agent recorded the basis for the severance and backfill position as the charge figure (card_0013) and the release status and hire (card_0054).", "type": "other", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0013 (full text of the EEOC charge) shows the severance figure the claim attributes to the charge: \"only four weeks of severance pay in the amount of $7,269.23, conditioned on my signing a release of all claims. I declined this offer,\" plus the replacement/backfill allegation. card_0054 shows the agent appending to process/notes.md the supporting facts it used for that position: \"Severance: 4 wks $7,269.23, conditioned on release; Harmon declined (Release=N)\" (release status) and \"Replacement: David Alejandro Ruiz, Hispanic male, age 36, hired 3/10/25, $97,000\" (the hire), and its planned section \"G severance/replacement.\" So the agent indeed recorded the severance figure from the charg … [121 more characters]"}, {"id": "c63", "claim": "For the pattern evidence (CA-4), the draft argues that there were 16 performance terminations from 2022 to 2025, across races and sexes, and five formal PIPs.", "type": "decision", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0060 shows the draft's CA-4 section (\"Company-Wide Performance-Management and Termination Records\") stating performance-related terminations totaled 16 (2022–March 2025; 4 per year), broken down 10 Caucasian / 3 African American / 2 Hispanic / 1 Asian American and 10 male / 6 female, framed as \"applied across the workforce without regard to race or sex,\" and that 5 employees were placed on formal PIPs (3 passed, 2 failed). card_0058 shows the figures cross-checked (\"16 perf terminations 2022-25: 10 Cau / 3 AFam / 2 Hisp / 1 AAm; 10 M / 6 F\"; \"5 formal PIPs (3 retained/passed, 2 failed->term)\"). card_0067 shows a final check confirming the \"16\" statistic and \"five formal PIPs\" match the … [159 more characters]"}, {"id": "c64", "claim": "The agent recorded the basis for the pattern evidence position as the summary tab (card_0046) and notes (card_0058).", "type": "other", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0046 shows the agent reading the \"Summary Statistics\" sheet of vpm-termination-log.xlsx (16 total performance terminations 2022-25; 10 Cau / 3 AFam / 2 Hisp / 1 AAm; 10 M / 6 F, plus PIP outcome counts), i.e. the summary tab. card_0058 shows the agent appending to process/notes.md the pattern line \"16 perf terminations 2022-25: 10 Cau / 3 AFam / 2 Hisp / 1 AAm; 10 M / 6 F. (term log Summary Statistics)\" — the note recording the pattern evidence with the summary tab explicitly cited as its basis. This matches the claim that the agent recorded the basis for the pattern evidence position as the summary tab (card_0046) and notes (card_0058)."}, {"id": "c65", "claim": "On the charge's claim that no similarly situated male was placed on a PIP, the draft cites a 2022 PIP for a CNC Operator II in a different position (terminated January 2023).", "type": "decision", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0060 shows the draft position statement's CA-4 text in the build script: of the 5 formal PIPs, \"2 failed their PIPs and were terminated — including a Caucasian male employee (CNC Operator II) whose 2022 PIP failure led to his January 2023 termination.\" card_0081 confirms the final draft contains this rebuttal against the charge's \"no similarly situated male employee was subjected to a PIP\" statement (Grady, \"CNC Operator II,\" PIP started 10/3/2022, terminated 1/6/2023), and the agent explicitly notes Grady was in a different position. Both elements of the claim — the 2022 CNC Operator II PIP cited to rebut the similarly-situated-male allegation, and the January 2023 termination — appear … [11 more characters]"}, {"id": "c66", "claim": "The agent noted that the CNC Operator II's position differs and that his PIP predates the charged period, and judged the point usable as a showing that PIPs are not unique to Harmon (card_0081).", "type": "decision", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0081 contains the agent's reasoning that matches all three elements of the claim: it identifies Grady as the \"CNC Operator II\" whose PIP ran 10/3/2022–1/1/2023, explicitly notes \"Grady's PIP is 2022, not 'during this period'\" (i.e., it predates the charged period of Sept 6, 2024 – Jan 17, 2025), notes \"Grady was a different position,\" and judges the point usable — \"the company-wide PIP practice shows it is not unique. This is fine as a draft for a position statement.\""}, {"id": "c67", "claim": "The final state of the work is a draft position statement delivered as a single .docx left in place in the workspace; the draft lists Exhibits A-J and CA-1 to CA-5, but no exhibit documents were attached to it, and it is an unreviewed draft.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0085 (terminal) confirms the final deliverable is the single vpm-position-statement.docx left in place at the requested path (\"the only artifact at the requested path\"), and the agent consistently refers to it as \"the draft\" (e.g., \"the draft uses 'Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.'\"); no card shows any human/company review step, so \"unreviewed draft\" in the company's name (letterhead, Milliken VP-HR signature, counsel of record per card_0082/card_0085) matches. card_0082's programmatic check on the .docx prints PASS for \"CA-1..CA-5 present\" and \"exhibits A..J\"; card_0084 shows the rendered text citing them inline (Exhibit G/H/I, Confidential Attachment CA-4), i.e., they are list … [279 more characters]"}, {"id": "c68", "claim": "The draft has had no review by counsel or by anyone other than the agent.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The trajectory (85 cards across all 88 turns, fully enumerated in the index) contains only agent-performed actions: drafting vpm-position-statement.docx and self-verification. card_0083 and card_0084 show the agent alone re-checking math, structure, and name leakage, and card_0085 (terminal) shows the run ending with the agent's own checks passing and the deliverable handed to the user — no card anywhere records counsel or any other person reviewing the draft. card_0053 shows counsel (Blackwell, Thayer & Oakes LLP) appearing only because the agent itself chose to name them in the draft's signature block, not as an actual reviewer. With the trace this dense, a review step would have appeared … [32 more characters]"}, {"id": "c69", "claim": "The letter is written for the signature of VPM's Vice President of Human Resources, with outside counsel named.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0082 runs a programmatic check on the built vpm-position-statement.docx and shows \"PASS signature block Milliken\" (both \"Sandra Milliken\" and \"Vice President of Human Resources\" present in the document text) and \"PASS counsel\" (\"Blackwell, Thayer & Oakes\" present), with ALL PASS overall. card_0085, the terminal summary, records the delivered letter as \"signed by Sandra Milliken, VP HR, with counsel Blackwell, Thayer & Oakes LLP.\" card_0053 shows the decision behind it: the review compilation names Blackwell, Thayer & Oakes LLP as the company's counsel, and the agent chose a signature block for Sandra Milliken, VP of Human Resources, with that counsel noted. The claim that the letter is … [77 more characters]"}, {"id": "c70", "claim": "Several arguments in the draft are judgment calls, including the rebuttal to the claim that the same HR staff investigated and approved the termination.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0060 (the draft build script) contains the exact rebuttal the claim names: in Section III.E the draft states that the Charge's suggestion that the same \"Human Resources personnel\" who \"investigated\" Harmon's complaint \"would later approve my termination\" mischaracterizes the process, because the investigation only recommended proceeding with performance management and the termination was \"independently approved at the CEO level.\" card_0053 shows the agent identifying this point as a weakness in VPM's case (\"Harmon alleges Milliken was 'the same HR personnel who would later approve my termination.' This is a potential weakness ... that I should acknowledge/handle carefully\") and choosing … [104 more characters]"}, {"id": "c71", "claim": "The Summary Statistics tab of the workbook counts 13 backfills (7 Caucasian, 2 African American, 2 Hispanic, 2 Asian American; 8 male, 5 female).", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0046 prints the Summary Statistics tab of vpm-termination-log.xlsx in full: \"Positions Backfilled | 3 | 4 | 3 | 3 | 13\", \"REPLACEMENT HIRES BY RACE — Caucasian 7 / African American 2 / Hispanic 2 / Asian American 2\", and \"REPLACEMENT HIRES BY SEX — Male 8 / Female 5\". Every figure in the claim (13 backfills; 7/2/2/2 by race; 8/5 by sex) matches the tab exactly. card_0044 confirms the workbook contains a \"Summary Statistics\" sheet. (The Replacement Hires tab in card_0044 shows different counts — 12 completed, Asian American = 1, Female = 4 — but the claim is scoped to the Summary Statistics tab, which matches.)"}, {"id": "c72", "claim": "The Replacement Hires tab of the same workbook states a total of 12 completed backfills (race 7/2/2/1; 8 male, 4 female), but its rows list 13 completed hires, including the Harmon replacement (record 14).", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0044 (full dump) shows the Replacement Hires tab of vpm-termination-log.xlsx stating \"Total Positions Backfilled (completed): 12\", \"Replacement Hires by Race: Caucasian = 7; African American = 2; Hispanic = 2; Asian American = 1\", and \"Replacement Hires by Sex: Male = 8; Female = 4\" — exactly the figures the claim attributes to the tab. The same tab's rows, however, contain 13 hires with actual replacement names and hire dates (records 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12, 13, 14, 16); only record 15 (Sellers) is \"TBD — Position Posted\". Row 14 is the Harmon replacement (David Alejandro Ruiz, hired 03/10/2025), which card_0046 confirms is record #14, Denise Latrice Harmon. The claimed interna … [103 more characters]"}, {"id": "c73", "claim": "The agent's own row count gave 8 Caucasian hires, so the race splits conflict as well.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0069 records the agent's verification pass, in which its own row-by-row count of the Replacement Hires tab (records 2,3,6,7,8,10,13,16) gives \"Caucasian ... That's 8 Caucasian\" out of 13 completed backfills, while both tab summaries state Caucasian = 7. The agent explicitly concludes the two source tabs disagree on the race breakdown, which is exactly the conflict the claim reports."}, {"id": "c74", "claim": "The final checklist cites the Replacement Hires tab for the 13 backfill figure, which the section says is a miscitation.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0081 contains the agent's final fact-check checklist, whose item 6 reads: \"13 completed backfills, 7 white, 2 Black, 2 Hispanic, 2 Asian American; 8 male, 5 female\" ✓ (replacement-hire tab) — i.e., the checklist does cite the Replacement Hires tab for the 13 figure. The workbook evidence shows that citation is wrong: the Replacement Hires tab itself (full text of card_0044's dump) lists only 12 completed replacement rows and its own footer states \"Total Positions Backfilled (completed): 12\" with \"Asian American = 1\" and \"Female = 4\". The 13 figure (7 Cau / 2 AFam / 2 Hisp / 2 AAm; 8 male / 5 female) appears in the Summary Statistics sheet's REPLACEMENT HIRE SUMMARY block (card_0046 full … [150 more characters]"}, {"id": "c75", "claim": "CA-4 of the draft states 13 backfills as fact.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim is about the draft position statement's CA-4 section, not about the report itself. card_0067 shows the agent reviewing the built document and confirming \"CA-4: 'of 13 completed backfills, 7 were Caucasian, 2 African American, 2 Hispanic, and 2 Asian American; 8 male and 5 female' — matches the replacement hire summary (13 completed; race 7/2/2/2; gender 8/5). OK.\" card_0069 shows the agent acknowledging \"My CA-4 currently says 'of 13 completed backfills...'\" and deciding to keep the flat figure from the Summary Statistics tab rather than soften it. card_0081's final post-build check lists the CA-4 text \"13 completed backfills, 7 white, 2 Black, 2 Hispanic, 2 Asian American; 8 male, … [116 more characters]"}, {"id": "c76", "claim": "The conflicting backfill figures must be reconciled against HR records before filing.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The cards show the conflicting backfill figures were resolved in-session, not left as work pending reconciliation before filing. card_0069 records the agent finding the two termination-log tabs disagree (Replacement Hires tab: 12 completed / 1 Asian American; Summary Statistics tab: 13 completed / 7/2/2/2) and explicitly deciding the Summary Statistics tab is internally consistent and traceable, concluding \"I'll keep it\" — i.e., the discrepancy was adjudicated, not flagged as an open item. card_0067's figure check confirms the CA-4 backfill numbers \"matches the replacement hire summary (13 completed; race 7/2/2/2; gender 8/5). OK.\", and card_0081's final spot-check marks the same figures ver … [293 more characters]"}, {"id": "c77", "claim": "The company name conflicts across sources: the charge and transmittal use Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.; the review form, the PIP and the EEO policy use Vanguard Precision Manufacturing, Inc.; the termination memo uses Valiant Precision Manufacturing, Inc.; the emails use a vanguardprecision.com domain; and the draft uses Saxonbrook.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim's per-document name mapping matches the trace. card_0016 shows the transmittal letterhead reading \"Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.\"; card_0053 (turn 56) lists the exact per-file letterheads: charge and transmittal use \"Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.\"; harmon-performance-reviews.docx's review form says \"VANGUARD PRECISION MANUFACTURING, INC.\"; harmon-pip-october-2024.docx and vpm-eeo-policy.docx headers say \"VANGUARD PRECISION MANUFACTURING, INC.\"; poole-termination-memo.docx says \"VALIANT PRECISION MANUFACTURING, INC.\" card_0040 shows both .eml emails on the vanguardprecision.com domain (mpoole@ / smilliken@vanguardprecision.com, mail.vanguardprecision.com). card … [167 more characters]"}, {"id": "c78", "claim": "The legal company name must be confirmed, because the exhibits carry the other names.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The premise half is real: the source documents do carry conflicting letterhead names (card_0053 lists Vanguard on the PIP/EEo-policy/review forms, Valiant on the termination memo, Saxonbrook on the EEOC charge and transmittal). But the claim frames the legal name as unresolved work that \"must be confirmed,\" and the trace shows the opposite: the agent explicitly decided the name was settled by the authoritative EEOC documents, choosing \"Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.\" and treating the Vanguard/Valiant variants as a distractor (card_0053: \"I should use the authoritative name from the EEOC charge and transmittal\"). Card_0084 shows it then verified the final document had zero Vanguard/ … [311 more characters]"}, {"id": "c79", "claim": "Harmon's employee ID conflicts, with four records giving four different IDs ending in 0247, 0284, 0847 and 1087.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The cards show Harmon's employee ID as four different values across four record types, exactly as the claim states. The performance-reviews material shows \"Employee ID: VPM-2019-0247\" (card_0016, also card_0020/0024/0026); the October 2024 PIP document shows \"VPM-2019-0284\" (card_0028); the pip-tracking-log.xlsx PIP Summary sheet shows \"Employee ID | VPM-2019-0847\" (card_0042); and the vpm-termination-log.xlsx Performance Terminations row for Denise Latrice Harmon shows \"Employee ID: VPM-1087\" (card_0046). The agent itself enumerates all four IDs and their inconsistency in its reasoning (card_0053): performance reviews VPM-2019-0247, PIP VPM-2019-0284, pip-tracking-log VPM-2019-0847, termina … [129 more characters]"}, {"id": "c80", "claim": "The draft cites the 0247 form in CA-5.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0060 (the build script that generates the draft vpm-position-statement.docx) shows the CA-5 section — heading('CONFIDENTIAL ATTACHMENT CA-5: RECORDS MAINTAINED BY VPM IN THIS MATTER') — whose first bullet is \"Denise L. Harmon personnel file (VPM-2019-0247), including all annual performance reviews FY2020–FY2024 ...\", i.e., the 0247 form/ID is cited in CA-5 as the claim states. card_0082's verification run confirms CA-1..CA-5 are all present in the final document (PASS), so the CA-5 section with that citation was in the draft."}, {"id": "c81", "claim": "The date of the Sellers coaching plan conflicts: the investigation summary dates it to July 2024 while the termination-log note gives 11/01/2024, and the draft says only \"during 2024\".", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0047 shows the agent reading the sources and noting the exact conflict: \"Sellers' coaching plan started on 11/01/2024 (in the investigation summary it says July 2024 — a discrepancy to watch out for)\", i.e. the investigation summary dates it July 2024 while the termination-log note gives 11/01/2024. card_0054 shows the process notes recording the same conflict (\"Sellers coaching-plan start: investigation=\\\"July 2024\\\" vs term-log note=\\\"11/01/2024\\\"\") and the decision to phrase the date as \"during 2024\" in the draft, which is where the report's \"during 2024\" wording comes from."}, {"id": "c82", "claim": "The letter is dated April 18, 2025, the due date in the transmittal, while the notes are dated 2026-10-05 and today is 2026-10-22.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "unverifiable", "why": "Three of the four sub-claims are shown by the cards: the transmittal requires the position statement \"no later than April 18, 2025\" (card_0011), the process notes are written with \"Date started: 2026-10-05\" and a later \"FINAL VERIFICATION (2026-10-05, post-build)\" section (card_0015, card_0084), and the built letter carries the date \"April 18, 2025\" (card_0064, echoed in the terminal card). However, the claim's remaining assertion — \"today is 2026-10-22\" — is a statement about the session date outside the saved evidence: I searched all cards and full texts for any date command, \"2026-10-22\", or \"October 22\", and none appears; the only 2026 dates in the trace are the 2026-10-05 notes dates. T … [158 more characters]"}, {"id": "c83", "claim": "The record does not show the agent considering whether the filing deadline still applies or what date the letter should carry, so the letter's date may be stale.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The claim says the record does not show the agent considering whether the deadline still applies or what date the letter should carry. The record shows the opposite on the date question: card_0053 contains explicit deliberation (\"should I date it the due date? A position statement is typically dated when submitted. I'll use April 18, 2025 (the deadline) to be safe and realistic\"), card_0054 logs the decision in process notes (\"date 4/18/25 (due date)\"), and card_0085 confirms the delivered letter is dated April 18, 2025. card_0015 also shows the agent tracked both the session date (2026-10-05) and the 2025-04-18 due date in its notes, so the stale-date tension was within the agent's knowledg … [145 more characters]"}, {"id": "c84", "claim": "CA-5 of the draft says VPM \"will produce\" its records on request, which is a commitment the company must be willing to make.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0060 contains the draft-building script that writes CA-5 of the position statement: \"CONFIDENTIAL ATTACHMENT CA-5: RECORDS MAINTAINED BY VPM IN THIS MATTER\" with the sentence \"VPM maintains the following records in the confidential HR investigation file and the employee personnel file, and will produce them to the EEOC upon request.\" This shows CA-5 of the draft does say VPM \"will produce\" its records on request, as the claim states; the latter half of the claim is the report's own (uncontroversial) characterization of that commitment."}, {"id": "c85", "claim": "The closing message of the draft says \"All 10 source files read in full\" and that every date, figure, name and quote traces to a source.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0085 is the terminal (turn 88) card carrying the agent's final closing message to the user, and it states verbatim: \"All 10 source files read in full; every date, figure, name, and quote in the draft traced to a source (spot-check script on ~30 key facts...)\". That matches the claim exactly. card_0084 corroborates the underlying note appended to process/notes.md: \"Sources: all 10 workspace files read in full\", and the earlier cards (card_0018's conversion of all .docx files, the xlsx inspections, etc.) are consistent with 10 files having been read."}, {"id": "c86", "claim": "The workspace held 11 files.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0002 shows `find /app/workspace -type f | head -100` listing exactly 11 files (5 docx, 2 xlsx, 2 eml, 2 docx for harmon/poole, plus eeoc-charge-410-2025-01847.docx), and the listing terminates at the `---` separator, so it is not truncated by head -100. This directly supports \"The workspace held 11 files.\""}, {"id": "c87", "claim": "Several source-file reads were cut off by the tool, so some sources were read in several passes.", "type": "runtime_failure", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0011 shows a tool-truncated source read (\"Warning: truncated output\") of the EEOC charge, and the agent's own reasoning (card_0017) states \"The output is truncated partway through. I need to read these documents in more detail. Let me dump each document to a text file.\" The subsequent multi-pass reads follow directly: cards 0020/0024/0026 read the same file (harmon-performance-reviews.txt) in three separate sed passes over line ranges 1-150, 253-336, and 337-475. Grep over the remaining cards shows the same pattern for other sources (e.g., card_0012 and card_0029 note truncated charge and PIP reads, and many tool outputs carry \"Warning: truncated output\"). The claim that several reads w … [92 more characters]"}, {"id": "c88", "claim": "The agent recovered from the cut-off reads by reading some sources in several passes, and the EEO policy's retaliation section was read separately.", "type": "recovery", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The trace shows cut-off reads and multi-pass recovery: outputs were repeatedly truncated (cards 0020/0024/0026/0028 etc. all end with \"truncated to 800 of ...\"), and card_0017's reasoning explicitly says \"The output is truncated partway through. I need to read these documents in more detail,\" leading to sed-based multi-pass reads, e.g. harmon-performance-reviews.txt read in passes 1,150p (card_0020), 253,336p (card_0024), and 337,475p (card_0026). For the EEO policy, card_0052 shows an initial read (sed '1,120p' vpm-eeo-policy.txt, output truncated at 800 of 10193 chars); card_0055's reasoning then states the agent would separately check \"section 5 of the EEO policy (the retaliation section) … [275 more characters]"}, {"id": "c89", "claim": "The items the closing message traces to sources are the spot checks the agent ran, not a verification that every statement in the draft traces to a source.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0085 shows the closing message's \"Verification performed\" section, which asserts \"every date, figure, name, and quote in the draft traced to a source\" but immediately qualifies it as a \"spot-check script on ~30 key facts\" (ratings, NCR figures, $7,269.23, NAF-2024-NC-0042, termination counts, etc.). card_0066 shows the actual checks run: a \"KEY FACT SPOT-CHECKS (grep count in my doc)\" loop over ~28 hardcoded fact patterns, plus structure checks (card_0082) — a sample of key items, not an exhaustive statement-by-statement trace of the whole draft. card_0058 shows the notes record these as cross-checked \"key figures\" rather than full-document trace verification. So the report's characteri … [153 more characters]"}, {"id": "c90", "claim": "The agent made several choices without asking, including the respondent name, the employee ID, the Sellers date, the choice of the Summary Statistics tally over the Replacement Hires tab, and the framing of the investigation as thorough and in good faith.", "type": "decision", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim is that the agent made these content choices on its own, and the cards show each one being decided unilaterally by the agent (no user question is recorded anywhere in the 85-card trace): (1) respondent name — card_0039 shows the agent noticing brand-name inconsistencies (Saxonbrook vs Vanguard vs Valiant) across letterheads and deciding \"I'll use the name listed in the EEOC charge/transmittal (Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.)\"; (2) employee ID — card_0053 shows the agent aware of the conflicting IDs (VPM-2019-0247 vs VPM-2019-0284) and deciding to use VPM-2019-0247 from the performance review compilation as the most \"official\"; (3) the Sellers date — card_0053 shows the ag … [996 more characters]"}, {"id": "c91", "claim": "The closing message mentions some of the choices made without asking, but not the backfill conflict.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0085 is the terminal turn carrying the closing message. It names several judgment calls made without consulting the user: using Saxonbrook (per the charge/transmittal) rather than the conflicting internal letterhead names Vanguard/Valiant, and documenting distractor inconsistencies (employee IDs, Sellers coaching-plan date) in process/notes.md, plus the month-math and PIP-target fixes. It never mentions the backfill data conflict. card_0069 shows that conflict existed and was silently resolved: the agent found the Replacement Hires tab (12 completed, 7 Cau/1 AAm, M=8/F=4) contradicting the Summary Statistics tab (13 completed, 7 Cau/2 AAm, M=8/F=5) and its own row count (13 completed, 8 … [218 more characters]"}, {"id": "c92", "claim": "The agent installed python-docx and openpyxl with pip.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0007 shows both imports (openpyxl, docx) failing and no packages in pip list, i.e. they were not yet installed; card_0009 shows the agent running `pip install python-docx openpyxl` which completed with exit code 0. This directly matches the claim that the agent installed python-docx and openpyxl with pip."}, {"id": "c93", "claim": "The agent created process/notes.md in the workspace.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0005 shows the agent running `mkdir -p /app/workspace/process` (exit code 0), and card_0015 shows `cat > /app/workspace/process/notes.md <<'EOF' ... EOF` writing the notes file, whose output was \"done\" with exit code 0. The file was created in the workspace as stated."}, {"id": "c94", "claim": "Harmon's hire date (Aug 19, 2019), final salary ($94,500), and grade (E-7) are visible in the source output of card_0046 (termination log).", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 6, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0046 is the tool output of reading the \"Performance Terminations\" sheet of vpm-termination-log.xlsx, and its Harmon record shows exactly the values the claim names: \"Hire Date: 08/19/2019\", \"Final Annual Salary: $94,500\", and \"Salary Grade: E-7\" (Employee Name: Denise Latrice Harmon)."}, {"id": "c95", "claim": "The PIP ran from Oct 14, 2024 to Jan 12, 2025, Harmon was terminated Jan 17, 2025, and 3 of 4 PIP objectives were missed, all visible in card_0046 source output.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 6, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0046 (openpyxl dump of vpm-termination-log.xlsx, \"Performance Terminations\" row for Denise Latrice Harmon) shows exactly the claimed facts: PIP Start Date 10/14/2024, PIP End Date 01/12/2025, PIP Outcome \"Failed — Did not meet 3 of 4 objectives\", and Termination Date 01/17/2025. All three elements of the claim appear in card_0046's source output as stated."}, {"id": "c96", "claim": "The FY2024 rating of 2.4 dated Sept 6, 2024 is visible in the source output of card_0030 and card_0034.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 6, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0030's grep output of harmon-pip-october-2024.txt shows the fiscal year 2024 annual performance review conducted September 6, 2024 with an overall rating of 2.4 (\"Needs Improvement\") on the five-point scale. card_0034's sed output of hr-investigation-summary.txt shows \"FY2024 ratings among Harmon (2.4), Yamashiro (3.1), and Sellers (2.8)\" and \"The overall rating of 2.4 was consistent with the documented performance metrics.\" Both named cards visibly contain the FY2024 rating of 2.4 in their source output, as the claim states."}, {"id": "c97", "claim": "NCR closure took 21.7 days in the review period and 14.8 days under the PIP, visible in the source output of card_0034 and card_0048.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 6, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0034 (hr-investigation-summary.txt) states \"Harmon's NCR closure time average of 21.7 business days was the highest among the three managers\" during the review period. card_0048's output from pip-tracking-log.xlsx (SUMMARY row, full card text) shows \"PIP actual result: 14.8 business days (32% reduction from baseline)\". Both figures appear exactly as the report passage claims, in the source output of the cited cards."}, {"id": "c98", "claim": "The supervisor's Dec 18, 2023 email reporting 23.4 days is visible in the source output of card_0040 and card_0076.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 6, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0040's output is a cat of the source file poole-qa-deficiency-email-dec-2023.eml, showing the email header \"Date: Mon, 18 Dec 2023\" from Marcus R. Poole to Sandra Milliken; its full output (cards/full/card_0040.txt) contains the NCR closure cycle time figure \"ballooned to 23.4 business days against our company target of 10 business days.\" card_0076's output likewise visibly contains the email's content: \"In an email dated December 18, 2023 to Sandra Milliken, VP of Human Resources, Poole reported that the department's trailing-twelve-month average NCR closure cycle time had 'ballooned to 23.4 business days'\". Both named cards therefore show the supervisor's Dec 18, 2023 email reporting … [53 more characters]"}, {"id": "c99", "claim": "The severance amount of $7,269.23 is visible in the charge text (card_0013) and in card_0054.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 6, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0013 shows the charge document (eeoc-charge-410-2025-01847.docx rendered to /tmp/charge.txt) containing \"Respondent offered me only four weeks of severance pay in the amount of $7,269.23, conditioned on my signing a release of all claims. I declined this offer.\" card_0054's notes append likewise records \"Severance: 4 wks $7,269.23, conditioned on release; Harmon declined (Release=N)\". Both cited cards show the amount as stated."}, {"id": "c100", "claim": "The arithmetic for the $7,269.23 severance was checked.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 6, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0058 appends a \"Verification of key figures (cross-checked)\" section to process/notes.md containing the explicit computation \"$7,269.23 = 94,500/52*4 = 7,269.23 (4 wks). Confirmed = charge + term log\" — the arithmetic check the claim describes, and it is numerically correct (94,500/52*4 = 7,269.23). card_0054 shows the supporting base figure was itself verified (salary $92,293 + $2,207 market adj = $94,500) and lists the severance as \"4 wks $7,269.23, conditioned on release,\" so the amount checked against the charge/term log matches the claim's figure."}, {"id": "c101", "claim": "There were 16 performance terminations from 2022 to 2025, visible in the card_0046 (summary tab) source output.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 6, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0046 shows the agent dumping the \"Summary Statistics\" sheet of vpm-termination-log.xlsx, whose first row reads \"Total Performance-Related Terminations | 4 | 4 | 4 | 4 | 16\" for columns 2022, 2023, 2024, and 2025 (YTD through Mar 2025). That is exactly the 16 performance terminations from 2022 to 2025 the report attributes to the card_0046 summary tab, so the claim matches the saved source output."}, {"id": "c102", "claim": "The completed-backfills figure is in conflict across sources: card_0046's Summary Statistics says 13 while card_0069's Replacement Hires tab states 12 but contains 13 rows.", "type": "other", "section": 6, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0046 shows the Summary Statistics tab of vpm-termination-log.xlsx stating \"Positions Backfilled ... Total 13\" (3+4+3+3 per year, and 13 in the total column). card_0069 (the agent's verification reasoning) records reading the Replacement Hires tab as stating \"Total Positions Backfilled (completed): 12\" (race breakdown 7/2/2/1, sex 8/4) while listing records 1,2,3,5,6,7,8,9,10,12,13,14,15,16, with record 15 (Sellers) marked \"TBD — Position Posted\" — i.e. 13 completed rows behind the stated 12. The report's passage faithfully describes this cross-source conflict."}, {"id": "c103", "claim": "The company name and employee ID are in conflict across sources, across cards 0016, 0024, 0028, 0038, 0040, 0052, 0042, and 0046.", "type": "other", "section": 6, "verdict": "supported", "why": "Every card the claim cites contains a company name and/or employee ID, and the values conflict across them. Company name: card_0016, card_0024, card_0028, card_0040, and card_0052 use \"Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.\" (mixed with \"VANGUARD PRECISION MANUFACTURING, INC.\" letterheads in card_0016/card_0024/card_0028/card_0052), while card_0038 (Poole termination memo) is letterheaded \"VALIANT PRECISION MANUFACTURING, INC.\" and names the company that way throughout. Employee ID for Denise Latrice Harmon: card_0016 and card_0024 show VPM-2019-0247; card_0028 (PIP) shows VPM-2019-0284; card_0042 (pip-tracking-log.xlsx PIP Summary) shows VPM-2019-0847; card_0046 (vpm-termination-log.xlsx) … [119 more characters]"}, {"id": "c104", "claim": "The report's claims rest on four allegations not being substantiated and seven individuals being interviewed, per card_0053 and card_0054 (the agent's notes).", "type": "other", "section": 6, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0053 (the HR investigation summary extraction) states \"7 individuals interviewed Sept 19–25, 2024: Harmon, Poole, Yamashiro, Sellers, Goss, Whitford, Solano\" and \"All four allegations found 'not substantiated'\". card_0054 (the agent's notes append) records the same: \"7 interviewed 9/19-9/25 (Harmon, Poole, Yamashiro, Sellers, Goss, Whitford, Solano). ALL 4 allegations NOT SUBSTANTIATED.\" Both cards are the agent's notes the report cites, and they carry the two figures (four allegations not substantiated, seven individuals interviewed) exactly as the report states."}, {"id": "c105", "claim": "The full summary behind the four-allegations/seven-interviews claim is not visible in the cards; the claim relies on the agent's notes.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 6, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim is a meta-statement about provenance: that the \"four allegations not substantiated; 7 individuals interviewed\" fact rests on the agent's notes because the full investigation summary is not visible in the cards. Both parts check out. card_0053 is an agent-reasoning step (empty command) that contains \"7 individuals interviewed Sept 19–25, 2024\" and \"All four allegations found 'not substantiated'\"; card_0054 appends the same facts (\"7 interviewed 9/19-9/25 ... ALL 4 allegations NOT SUBSTANTIATED\") to the agent's process/notes.md — so the report's cited sources (card_0053/card_0054) genuinely are the agent's notes, and the report's row correctly attributes the fact to them. Meanwhile t … [397 more characters]"}, {"id": "c106", "claim": "The report was generated from the workspace cards only, with no source outside the workspace consulted.", "type": "other", "section": 6, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The saved trace shows only workspace files as content sources. card_0002 inventories exactly the 11 files under /app/workspace; card_0013 and card_0018 show the agent deriving its text sources solely by pandoc-converting /app/workspace/*.docx into /tmp/src (a scratch copy, not an outside source); a grep across all 85 cards found no URLs, curl/wget, browser, or web-search commands anywhere in the trace (the only network-adjacent action, `pip install` in card_0009, installs local tooling, not a content source). card_0085's terminal summary states \"All 10 source files read in full; every date, figure, name, and quote in the draft traced to a source,\" consistent with generation from the workspac … [7 more characters]"}, {"id": "c107", "claim": "The Word file and notes file described in the report were not inspected directly.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 6, "verdict": "unverifiable", "why": "The claim is a meta-statement about which files (a Word file and a notes file) the report \"describes,\" and the only report text available is the claim passage itself, which does not name either file. I searched the trace for the referents: card_0002's find/ls of /app/workspace shows only 11 source files (docx, eml, xlsx) and no notes file anywhere in the workspace (the only notes file in the trace, process/notes.md, was created by the agent itself in card_0015/0054/0058/0084). There is no report artifact in the saved evidence that could show which Word file or notes file the report \"described,\" and no inspection of any unidentified \"notes file\" appears or would need to appear. The trace is d … [152 more characters]"}, {"id": "c108", "claim": "The figure shows a vertical timeline of 12 dated events in the Harmon matter, arranged in date order from Dec 18, 2023 to Apr 18, 2025, covering the FY2024 review, the written complaint, the investigation, the PIP, the termination, the EEOC charge, and the replacement hire.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "unverifiable", "why": "The claim is about the content of a report figure (sample_report.html#figure-1.svg: a vertical, date-ordered timeline of 12 dated events from Dec 18, 2023 to Apr 18, 2025). I searched all 85 source cards for any trace of this figure — no card writes, references, or renders sample_report.html, figure-1.svg, or any diagram/chart. The saved trajectory ends at card_0085 with the sole deliverable described as vpm-position-statement.docx; no figure-building step appears, so the figure's layout, event count, and ordering are outside the saved evidence and cannot be confirmed or denied. The underlying dates the claim names are individually traceable in the cards (card_0053: Poole email 12/18/2023, F … [224 more characters]"}, {"id": "c109", "claim": "The figure records that on Dec 18, 2023 a supervisor's email cited NCR closures at 23.4 days against a 10-day target.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The figure's dated event — Dec 18, 2023, supervisor's email citing NCR closures at 23.4 days against a 10-day target — is recorded in the source documents exactly as stated. card_0053 shows the agent noting, from the source materials, \"December 18, 2023 email to Milliken flagging QA deficiencies: NCR closure avg 23.4 business days vs 10-day target\" from new supervisor Marcus Poole (Director of Quality Systems). card_0054 shows the same fact carried into the verified process notes (\"Poole email 12/18/23 to Milliken: NCR closure 23.4 bd vs 10-day target\"). card_0069 and card_0081 quote the underlying email directly: \"the department average has ballooned to 23.4 business days against our compan … [470 more characters]"}, {"id": "c110", "claim": "The figure records that on Aug 22, 2024 an alleged \"keep up with the pace\" remark was made.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0013 (full text of eeoc-charge-410-2025-01847.docx) contains the passage: \"On or about August 22, 2024, during a group meeting, Mr. Poole stated, 'we need people who can keep up with the pace here,' while looking directly at me,\" and the Sept 12 complaint citation of the \"keep up with the pace\" comment. card_0011 shows the same charge content read from the source docx. The claim that the figure records an alleged \"keep up with the pace\" remark on Aug 22, 2024 is faithfully drawn from this source; the \"alleged\" framing matches, since the event is an allegation in the EEOC charge."}, {"id": "c111", "claim": "The figure records that on Sep 3, 2024 Harmon was allegedly excluded from a Northfield Aerospace meeting.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The figure file (sample_report.html#figure-1.svg) is not in the saved evidence, so the figure's own pixels cannot be inspected; the claim is judged on whether the recorded facts behind the figure are faithful. card_0011 shows the agent reading the EEOC charge (eeoc-charge-410-2025-01847.docx) directly. card_0015 shows the agent's notes recording, from that charge, the alleged events verbatim: \"9/3/24 exclusion from Northfield Aerospace meeting\" among Harmon's allegations. card_0057 (the agent's re-reading of the charge's Particulars) again lists \"Sept 3, 2024 - excluded from Northfield Aerospace meeting\" and labels it an allegation (\"Allegation 2 (Northfield meeting exclusion)\"), which the p … [149 more characters]"}, {"id": "c112", "claim": "The figure records that on Sep 6, 2024 the FY2024 review gave a rating of 2.4, six days before the complaint.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0022 (and card_0026, which reads the same FY2024 review section) shows the FY2024 Annual Performance Review for Harmon with Review Date September 6, 2024 and Overall Composite Rating 2.4 — Needs Improvement. card_0032 and card_0053 show the written complaint was received September 12, 2024 (Harmon's email to Milliken). September 12 is exactly six days after September 6, so the figure's recorded dating of the review (9/6/24, rating 2.4) and the \"six days before the complaint\" relationship match the source evidence."}, {"id": "c113", "claim": "The figure records that on Sep 12, 2024 Harmon submitted a written complaint of discrimination to the VP of HR.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0015 (notes from the EEOC charge/transmittal) records the alleged event \"9/12/24 written complaint to Sandra Milliken (VP HR)\", and card_0032 (the HR investigation summary source doc) confirms it: \"Investigation of Discrimination Complaint Filed by Denise Latrice Harmon ... Complaint Received: September 12, 2024\". card_0057 shows the agent cross-checking the same timeline item (\"Sept 12, 2024 - written complaint to Sandra Milliken (protected activity)\"). The figure's dated event — Sep 12, 2024 written discrimination complaint to the VP of HR — matches the source evidence on date, form, subject, and recipient."}, {"id": "c114", "claim": "The figure records that on Oct 4, 2024 the investigation concluded that all four allegations were not substantiated.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0053 (the agent's extraction from hr-investigation-summary) records \"Concluded October 4, 2024\" and lists each of the four allegations as \"Not substantiated\", summarized as \"All four allegations found 'not substantiated'\" — exactly what the figure claims. card_0032 shows the underlying source document (HR investigation summary) is dated and concluded October 4, 2024 and contains precisely four allegations. card_0058's process notes independently cross-check the timeline as \"investigation 9/16-10/4/24 (not substantiated)\". The figure's entry matches the source evidence as recorded."}, {"id": "c115", "claim": "The figure records that on Oct 14, 2024 a 90-day PIP was issued, 10 days after the investigation concluded and 32 days after the complaint.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0028 (full text of the PIP document) records \"Date of PIP Issuance: October 14, 2024\" and \"PIP Period: October 14, 2024 through January 12, 2025 (90 calendar days)\", confirming the 90-day PIP issued on Oct 14, 2024. card_0032 (HR investigation summary) records \"Complaint Received: September 12, 2024\" and \"Investigation Concluded: October 4, 2024\". The arithmetic holds: Oct 14 is 10 days after the Oct 4 conclusion and 32 days after the Sept 12 complaint (18 days remaining in September + 14 in October). The figure's dates match the source documents exactly."}, {"id": "c116", "claim": "The figure records that on Jan 12, 2025 the PIP ended and the termination log recorded 3 of 4 PIP objectives as missed.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0046 shows the agent reading vpm-termination-log.xlsx, where Record #14 (Denise Latrice Harmon) lists \"PIP End Date: 01/12/2025\" and \"PIP Outcome: Failed — Did not meet 3 of 4 objectives\" — exactly the two facts the figure claims (PIP ended Jan 12, 2025; termination log recorded 3 of 4 PIP objectives as missed). card_0058 shows the agent's cross-check notes confirming the same timeline (\"PIP 10/14/24 -> PIP ends 1/12/25 -> ... term 1/17/25\" and \"objective PIP failure (3/4 + partial 4th)\"), corroborating the figure against the source logs."}, {"id": "c117", "claim": "The figure records that termination became effective Jan 17, 2025, 5 days after the PIP ended.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The figure's claim that termination became effective Jan 17, 2025, 5 days after the PIP ended, matches the dates the agent recorded from the source documents. card_0015 (process notes written from the EEOC charge) states \"10/14/24 PIP 90-day; terminated 1/17/25 (5 days after PIP ended 1/12/25)\"; card_0053's timeline shows \"1/12/2025: PIP ends ... 1/17/2025: termination effective\" and \"Termination effective January 17, 2025\"; card_0057 repeats \"Jan 17, 2025 - terminated (5 days after PIP ended Jan 12, 2025)\"."}, {"id": "c118", "claim": "The figure records that on Feb 21, 2025 a charge was filed with the EEOC.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim states that the figure records a Feb 21, 2025 EEOC charge filing. The cards confirm the underlying fact: card_0011 shows the agent extracting eeoc-charge-410-2025-01847.docx (Charge No. 410-2025-01847) from the source documents; card_0053 records the charge facts the agent compiled from that document, including \"Charge No. 410-2025-01847\" and \"Filed February 21, 2025\"; card_0081 independently re-checks the date (\"Jan 17 → Feb 21 = 35 days\"). A grep of all cards for figure/sample_report/.svg artifacts finds none, so the figure file itself is not in the saved evidence, but the date/event the figure records is fully supported by the source-traceable record."}, {"id": "c119", "claim": "The figure records that on Mar 10, 2025 a replacement was hired at a salary of $97,000.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim that the figure records a replacement hired on Mar 10, 2025 at $97,000 matches the source evidence. card_0053 shows the termination log entry \"Position backfilled: David Alejandro Ruiz, Hispanic male, age 36, hired 3/10/25, starting salary $97,000\"; card_0047 independently notes the log records \"Ruiz's hiring as 03/10/2025\"; card_0059 shows the position statement stating \"The position was backfilled (David A. Ruiz, March 10, 2025)\"; and card_0066's spot-checks confirm both \"March 10, 2025\" and \"97,000\" appear in the final deliverable the figure is drawn from."}, {"id": "c120", "claim": "The figure records that the position statement was due on Apr 18, 2025, a date the figure notes comes from the EEOC transmittal.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim says the figure records that the position statement was due Apr 18, 2025 and attributes that date to the EEOC transmittal. The evidence shows the agent recorded precisely this fact with this provenance. card_0011's full text (the EEOC transmittal letter extracted via pandoc) states the Position Statement \"must be received by the EEOC Atlanta District Office no later than April 18, 2025 (forty-five calendar days from the date of this letter),\" so the date genuinely comes from the transmittal. card_0015 shows the agent's process notes listing under \"Key facts from transmittal\": \"Position statement due 2025-04-18 (45 days); to Investigator Paul D. Strickland,\" and card_0059 repeats th … [180 more characters]"}], "recoveries": [{"event": "event_001", "verdict": "adapted", "why": "card_0004 is the failure: the agent's update_plan tool call (a 4-step plan) was rejected at runtime with \"unsupported call: update_plan\" — the plan tool is unavailable in this environment. The agent never retried update_plan (a grep of the full card set shows that single call). Instead it changed approach: card_0005, the very next turn, proceeds with exec_command (mkdir -p /app/workspace/process and tooling checks), and card_0015 writes that same plan as persistent, file-based tracking — a notes.md file capturing the task, key facts, and the documents to review. The goal (set up and track a plan) was re-attempted with a different mechanism that avoids the reported cause (the unavailable upda … [60 more characters]"}, {"event": "event_002", "verdict": "adapted", "why": "card_0005 is the failing check: `python3 -c \"import docx, openpyxl\"` produced a\ntraceback (pandoc and python3 were present, but the modules were not).\ncard_0007 diagnoses the cause: pip list shows nothing matching docx/openpyxl\nand both separate imports traceback. Instead of re-running the same import,\nthe agent's next action (card_0009) is `pip install python-docx openpyxl`, a\nfix to the exact cause the failure reported. It then reads the .docx sources\nwith pandoc (card_0011) and later loads .xlsx files with openpyxl\n(card_0046), so the post-fix attempts use the working toolchain."}, {"event": "event_028", "verdict": "adapted", "why": "card_0062 is the failing verification command: `file vpm-position-statement.docx && pandoc ... | head -60` exited 127 with \"/bin/bash: line 1: file: command not found\", so the chained pandoc preview never ran. In card_0063 the agent acknowledges the missing `file` binary, and card_0064 re-attempts the same goal (verify the built docx is a real Office document and readable) with a changed approach: it drops `file` for a hex-magic-byte probe (`head -c 4 | xxd`) and runs the pandoc plain-text extraction directly, which succeeds and renders the document, unblocking the subsequent verification pass."}, {"event": "event_030", "verdict": "adapted", "why": "card_0064 is the failure: the magic-byte check `head -c 4 vpm-position-statement.docx | xxd | head -1` died with \"xxd: command not found\", so the byte-level check was never obtained (only the pandoc subcommand ran). The agent did not re-run xxd (or the earlier failed `file` probe from card_0062). Instead it changed the approach to the same verification goal: card_0065/card_0066 treat the successful full pandoc extraction (261 lines, all sections render, key facts grep-confirmed) as the validity evidence, and card_0082 later confirms the OOXML structure with a different tool entirely — python-docx `Document(\"vpm-position-statement.docx\")`, which only opens a structurally valid zip/OPC package … [128 more characters]"}]}, "branches": [], "reports": {"html": "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>Agent session report: EEOC position statement draft (Charge 410-2025-01847)<\/title>\n<style>\n:root { --ink:#111827; --muted:#4b5563; --line:#d1d5db; --accent:#1d4ed8; --warn:#9a3412; --panel:#f8fafc; }\n* { box-sizing: border-box; }\nbody { margin:0; font: 16px/1.55 system-ui, -apple-system, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, Arial, sans-serif; color:var(--ink); background:#ffffff; }\nmain { max-width: 920px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 32px 20px 64px; }\nh1 { font-size: 1.85rem; line-height: 1.25; margin: 0 0 10px; }\nh2 { font-size: 1.25rem; margin: 40px 0 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); padding-bottom: 6px; }\np, li { max-width: 80ch; }\n.lede { color: var(--muted); font-size: 1.05rem; }\n.meta { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem; }\n.callout { background: var(--panel); border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); padding: 14px 18px; margin: 20px 0; }\n.callout li { margin-bottom: 8px; }\ntable { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 12px 0 18px; font-size: 0.92rem; }\nth, td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: 7px 9px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }\nth { background: var(--panel); font-weight: 600; }\ncode { font-family: ui-monospace, Menlo, Consolas, monospace; font-size: 0.9em; background: #f3f4f6; padding: 0 3px; border-radius: 3px; }\nfigure { margin: 24px 0; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px; background: #ffffff; }\nfigure svg { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; }\nfigcaption { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.92rem; margin-top: 10px; }\n.numbered li { margin-bottom: 12px; }\nfooter { margin-top: 48px; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); padding-top: 12px; }\n@media print { body { font-size: 11pt; } h2 { break-after: avoid; } figure, table { break-inside: avoid; } }\n<\/style>\n<\/head>\n<body>\n<main>\n\n<header>\n<h1>What the agent did on the Harmon EEOC position statement<\/h1>\n<p class=\"lede\">A report on one recorded agent session. The agent drafted a position statement for Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc. (&ldquo;VPM&rdquo;) responding to Charge No. 410-2025-01847, filed by Denise Latrice Harmon, who alleges race and sex discrimination and retaliation.<\/p>\n<p class=\"meta\">Basis: the 85 step cards in the workspace (cards/, indexed in cards/index.md). Card references below look like card_0054.<\/p>\n<\/header>\n\n<h2>Bottom line<\/h2>\n<div class=\"callout\">\n<ul>\n<li>The agent produced one Word file, <code>/app/workspace/vpm-position-statement.docx<\/code>. It is a letter to the EEOC Atlanta District Office, dated April 18, 2025, that answers each allegation and asks for dismissal. Comparator data, witness accounts and company-wide statistics sit in a separate section marked not for disclosure to the charging party.<\/li>\n<li>The agent checked the file mechanically: text extraction, about 30 spot-checked names and figures, recalculated arithmetic, and 14 structural checks, all of which passed. It fixed several errors it found along the way, mostly date intervals.<\/li>\n<li>The record shows no sentence-by-sentence comparison of the draft against the sources and no legal review. The agent settled several conflicts in the source documents by its own choices and did not raise them with the user. One figure the draft states as fact (13 completed backfills) conflicts with another tab in the same workbook.<\/li>\n<li>The closing message calls the work &ldquo;complete and verified.&rdquo; The evidence supports a draft that was mechanically checked. It does not support a legal verification.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<h2>Scope and limits<\/h2>\n<p>This report uses only the step cards. The Word file and the notes file the agent created are not in this workspace, so their contents are described from the agent&rsquo;s own tool outputs. The cards do not include the original task prompt, so the agent&rsquo;s goal is inferred from its own notes and actions. Cards 53 and 57 are mostly the agent&rsquo;s long reasoning text, so this report relies on its commands, file writes and tool outputs rather than on that reasoning.<\/p>\n\n<h2>The materials<\/h2>\n<p>The workspace held 11 source files: seven Word documents, two emails and two spreadsheets (card_0002). The agent opened all of them, though not all in a single read.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>File<\/th><th>Kind<\/th><th>What it is<\/th><th>How the agent used it<\/th><\/tr><\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>eeoc-charge-410-2025-01847.docx<\/td><td>Word<\/td><td>The charge: parties, dates, particulars<\/td><td>Allegations and dates (cards 11&ndash;15)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>eeoc-transmittal-letter.docx<\/td><td>Word<\/td><td>EEOC transmittal: due date, investigator, confidentiality guidance<\/td><td>Date, addressee, split between disclosable body and confidential attachments (cards 11, 15)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>harmon-performance-reviews.docx<\/td><td>Word<\/td><td>FY2020&ndash;FY2024 review compilation, prepared at counsel&rsquo;s request<\/td><td>Rating history and the FY2024 rating of 2.4 (cards 16, 20&ndash;26)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>harmon-pip-october-2024.docx<\/td><td>Word<\/td><td>90-day performance improvement plan, Oct 14, 2024 to Jan 12, 2025<\/td><td>PIP objectives (cards 28&ndash;30)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>hr-investigation-summary.docx<\/td><td>Word<\/td><td>Internal investigation report, Oct 4, 2024<\/td><td>Findings on the four allegations; witness list (cards 32&ndash;36)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>poole-termination-memo.docx<\/td><td>Word<\/td><td>Termination recommendation, Jan 13, 2025<\/td><td>Termination reasons (card 38)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>vpm-eeo-policy.docx<\/td><td>Word<\/td><td>EEO and anti-retaliation policy HR-EEO-003<\/td><td>Policy citations (cards 52, 56)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>poole-qa-deficiency-email-dec-2023.eml<\/td><td>Email<\/td><td>Supervisor&rsquo;s QA assessment to HR, Dec 18, 2023<\/td><td>Documentation that predates the complaint (card 40)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>harmon-complaint-email-sept-2024.eml<\/td><td>Email<\/td><td>Harmon&rsquo;s written complaint to HR, Sept 12, 2024<\/td><td>Date of protected activity (card 40)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>pip-tracking-log.xlsx<\/td><td>Spreadsheet<\/td><td>Weekly NCR, corrective-action, audit and meeting data<\/td><td>PIP results (cards 42&ndash;50)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>vpm-termination-log.xlsx<\/td><td>Spreadsheet<\/td><td>2022&ndash;2025 terminations, summary tab, backfill tab<\/td><td>Pattern data and backfill counts (cards 44&ndash;46)<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n\n<h2>What the agent did, in order<\/h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Phase<\/th><th>Cards<\/th><th>What happened<\/th><\/tr><\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>1. Orientation<\/td><td>0001&ndash;0010<\/td><td>Listed the workspace and found pandoc installed but python-docx and openpyxl missing. Installed both with pip. An early plan-update call failed as an unsupported tool (card_0004).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>2. Reading the sources<\/td><td>0011&ndash;0056<\/td><td>Converted each Word file to text with pandoc and read the spreadsheets with openpyxl. Several outputs were cut off by the tool, so the agent re-read line ranges and searched with grep. It noted conflicts as it went.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>3. Fact notes<\/td><td>0015, 0054, 0058<\/td><td>Wrote <code>process/notes.md<\/code>: key facts, figures, a defense map for each allegation, and a list of source conflicts with the choice made for each.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>4. Drafting<\/td><td>0059&ndash;0060<\/td><td>Planned a letter with numbered sections, lettered exhibits, and confidential attachments CA-1 to CA-5. Wrote one Python script containing the full text and ran it to produce the .docx.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>5. Verification and repair<\/td><td>0061&ndash;0081<\/td><td>Opened the file with pandoc and python-docx, counted key strings, and re-did the arithmetic. Found wrong month intervals and fixed them over several rounds; one leftover phrase took three attempts (cards 0077&ndash;0080). Reworded a sentence about the PIP target that read as ambiguous.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>6. Final checks and report<\/td><td>0082&ndash;0085<\/td><td>Ran 14 structural checks (all passed), confirmed no &ldquo;Vanguard&rdquo; or &ldquo;Valiant&rdquo; text in the draft, appended a verification log to the notes, and wrote the closing message.<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n\n<h2>Why the agent made its main choices<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Respondent name.<\/strong> It used &ldquo;Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc.&rdquo; because the charge and the EEOC transmittal use that name, and several internal documents use other names (cards 0039, 0054).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Disclosure.<\/strong> It kept the letter body disclosable and moved sensitive material into a confidential attachment, following the transmittal&rsquo;s guidance (cards 0015, 0059).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Defense structure.<\/strong> It tied each allegation to a documented reason, and anchored the argument on the supervisor&rsquo;s December 18, 2023 email, which predates the September 12, 2024 complaint (card_0058).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Employee ID.<\/strong> It used the ID from the formal review compilation prepared for counsel and placed it only in the confidential attachment (cards 0054, 0060).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Date arithmetic.<\/strong> It corrected &ldquo;five months&rdquo; and &ldquo;nine months&rdquo; after computing the December 18, 2023 to September 6, 2024 interval as about 8.6 months (cards 0067, 0068, 0074, 0080).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h2>What the draft argues<\/h2>\n<p>The draft takes the company&rsquo;s position on each allegation. The table restates it with the basis the agent recorded.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Issue<\/th><th>Draft&rsquo;s position<\/th><th>Basis in the record<\/th><\/tr><\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>FY2024 rating of 2.4 (Sept 6, 2024)<\/td><td>Reflects documented metrics: NCR closure average of 21.7 business days against a 10-day standard, the most customer-audit findings of the three managers, and two overdue corrective actions. The deficiencies were written up on Dec 18, 2023, before any complaint. The five sub-scores average to 2.4.<\/td><td>Investigation summary (card_0034); notes (card_0054, card_0058). Sub-scores 3.0, 2.0, 2.5, 2.5, 2.0 average to 2.4.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Northfield meeting exclusion (Sept 3, 2024)<\/td><td>The meeting agenda was NDT-specific, not QA.<\/td><td>Agent&rsquo;s notes only (card_0058).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>&ldquo;Keep up with the pace&rdquo; remark (Aug 22, 2024)<\/td><td>A general remark about backlogs and audit deadlines. Two witnesses did not corroborate it as aimed at Harmon.<\/td><td>Agent&rsquo;s notes only (card_0058).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Leadership Development Program non-nomination<\/td><td>The program requires 18 months under the current supervisor. Harmon had about 11 months, and neither comparator was nominated either.<\/td><td>Program guideline quoted (card_0081); tenure figure from the investigation summary (card_0081).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>PIP (Oct 14, 2024)<\/td><td>Targets were measurable and communicated in advance. The 12-day NCR target was more lenient than the 10-day standard. Harmon reached 14.8 days, a 32% reduction, against the 45% reduction the target required.<\/td><td>PIP tracking figures (card_0048; card_0054); arithmetic (card_0058).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Termination (Jan 17, 2025)<\/td><td>Missed three of four PIP objectives. Termination required three levels of approval: supervisor recommendation, HR concurrence, and CEO approval.<\/td><td>Termination log outcome (card_0046); approval dates (card_0054).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Severance and backfill<\/td><td>Four weeks of pay, $7,269.23 ($94,500 divided by 52, times 4), was offered on condition of a release and was declined. A replacement was hired March 10, 2025 at $97,000.<\/td><td>Charge figure (card_0013); release status and hire (card_0054).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Pattern evidence (CA-4)<\/td><td>16 performance terminations from 2022 to 2025, across races and sexes, and five formal PIPs.<\/td><td>Summary tab (card_0046); notes (card_0058).<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>On the charge&rsquo;s claim that no similarly situated male was placed on a PIP, the draft cites a 2022 PIP for a CNC Operator II in a different position (terminated January 2023). The agent noted that the position differs, that the PIP predates the charged period, and judged the point usable as a showing that PIPs are not unique to Harmon (card_0081).<\/p>\n\n<h2>Timeline<\/h2>\n<figure data-pil-kind=\"diagram\" data-pil-caption=\"Dated events in the Harmon matter, drawn from the source documents as the agent recorded them\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 760 480\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"tl-title tl-desc\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"system-ui, Segoe UI, Roboto, Arial, sans-serif\">\n<title id=\"tl-title\">Dated events in the Harmon matter<\/title>\n<desc id=\"tl-desc\">Twelve dated events from December 18, 2023 to April 18, 2025, in date order, showing the review, complaint, investigation, PIP, termination, charge and replacement hire.<\/desc>\n<line x1=\"150\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"462\" stroke=\"#9ca3af\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<g font-size=\"14\">\n<circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"52\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#4b5563\"/>\n<text x=\"134\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#111827\">Dec 18, 2023<\/text>\n<text x=\"166\" y=\"57\" fill=\"#111827\">Supervisor&#8217;s email: NCR closures at 23.4 days vs. 10-day target<\/text>\n\n<circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"88\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"134\" y=\"93\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#111827\">Aug 22, 2024<\/text>\n<text x=\"166\" y=\"93\" fill=\"#111827\">Alleged &#8220;keep up with the pace&#8221; remark<\/text>\n\n<circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"124\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"134\" y=\"129\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#111827\">Sep 3, 2024<\/text>\n<text x=\"166\" y=\"129\" fill=\"#111827\">Alleged exclusion from Northfield Aerospace meeting<\/text>\n\n<circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"160\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#9a3412\"/>\n<text x=\"134\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#111827\">Sep 6, 2024<\/text>\n<text x=\"166\" y=\"165\" fill=\"#111827\">FY2024 review: rating 2.4, six days before the complaint<\/text>\n\n<circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"196\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#1d4ed8\"/>\n<text x=\"134\" y=\"201\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#111827\">Sep 12, 2024<\/text>\n<text x=\"166\" y=\"201\" fill=\"#111827\">Harmon&#8217;s written complaint of discrimination to VP of HR<\/text>\n\n<circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"232\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#1d4ed8\"/>\n<text x=\"134\" y=\"237\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#111827\">Oct 4, 2024<\/text>\n<text x=\"166\" y=\"237\" fill=\"#111827\">Investigation concludes: all four allegations not substantiated<\/text>\n\n<circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"268\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#9a3412\"/>\n<text x=\"134\" y=\"273\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#111827\">Oct 14, 2024<\/text>\n<text x=\"166\" y=\"273\" fill=\"#111827\">90-day PIP issued: 10 days after investigation, 32 after complaint<\/text>\n\n<circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"304\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#9a3412\"/>\n<text x=\"134\" y=\"309\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#111827\">Jan 12, 2025<\/text>\n<text x=\"166\" y=\"309\" fill=\"#111827\">PIP ends; termination log records 3 of 4 objectives missed<\/text>\n\n<circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"340\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#9a3412\"/>\n<text x=\"134\" y=\"345\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#111827\">Jan 17, 2025<\/text>\n<text x=\"166\" y=\"345\" fill=\"#111827\">Termination effective, 5 days after PIP ends<\/text>\n\n<circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"376\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#1d4ed8\"/>\n<text x=\"134\" y=\"381\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#111827\">Feb 21, 2025<\/text>\n<text x=\"166\" y=\"381\" fill=\"#111827\">Charge filed with the EEOC<\/text>\n\n<circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"412\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#9a3412\"/>\n<text x=\"134\" y=\"417\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#111827\">Mar 10, 2025<\/text>\n<text x=\"166\" y=\"417\" fill=\"#111827\">Replacement hired at $97,000<\/text>\n\n<circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"448\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#1d4ed8\"/>\n<text x=\"134\" y=\"453\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#111827\">Apr 18, 2025<\/text>\n<text x=\"166\" y=\"453\" fill=\"#111827\">Position statement due (date in the EEOC transmittal)<\/text>\n<\/g>\n<\/svg>\n<figcaption>Filled grey: pre-complaint documentation. Hollow circles: allegations by the charging party, not findings. Orange-brown: employer actions affecting Harmon (review, PIP, termination, replacement hire). Blue: complaint, investigation, charge, or deadline. The Aug 22 and Sep 3 items are allegations; the rest are dates recorded in the source documents (cards 0015, 0038, 0046, 0054).<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n\n<h2>How the work was checked<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Extraction.<\/strong> The extracted text runs to 261 lines, and python-docx opens the file with 132 paragraphs (cards 0066, 0070, 0085). The <code>file<\/code> and <code>xxd<\/code> tools were not installed, so the agent relied on python-docx instead (cards 0062, 0064).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Spot checks.<\/strong> About 30 strings were counted in the extracted text, including the charge number, key figures, names and a citation. The counts were re-run after later edits (cards 0066, 0084).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Arithmetic.<\/strong> Severance, percentage reductions and day intervals were recomputed and found consistent (cards 0058, 0081, 0084).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Quotations.<\/strong> Exact matches were recorded for a handful of quoted passages from the investigation summary, the EEO policy and the LDP guideline (card_0081). Quotation checks were not done across the whole draft.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Structure.<\/strong> 14 checks covering addressee, charge number, each allegation, the confidential marking, the exhibit list and the signature block all passed (card_0082).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Not done.<\/strong> No sentence-by-sentence comparison with the sources. No review of the legal arguments. No check of whether the April 18, 2025 date still applies. The agent described the readability check as &ldquo;the test the evaluator uses&rdquo; (card_0065), and the record contains no other acceptance criteria.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h2>What you should know before using the draft<\/h2>\n<ol class=\"numbered\">\n<li><strong>It is an unreviewed draft in a company&rsquo;s name.<\/strong> The letter is written for the signature of VPM&rsquo;s Vice President of Human Resources, with outside counsel named. The record shows no review by counsel or by anyone other than the agent. Several arguments are judgment calls, including the rebuttal to the claim that the same HR staff investigated and approved the termination.<\/li>\n<li><strong>The backfill figures conflict.<\/strong> The Summary Statistics tab counts 13 backfills (race 7 Caucasian, 2 African American, 2 Hispanic, 2 Asian American; 8 male, 5 female). The Replacement Hires tab of the same workbook states a total of 12 completed backfills (race 7/2/2/1; 8 male, 4 female), but its rows list 13 completed hires, including the Harmon replacement (record 14). The agent&rsquo;s own row count gave 8 Caucasian hires, so the race splits conflict as well (card_0069). The final checklist cites the Replacement Hires tab for the 13 figure (card_0081), which is a miscitation. CA-4 states 13 as fact. Reconcile against HR records before filing.<\/li>\n<li><strong>The company name conflicts across sources.<\/strong> The charge and transmittal use Saxonbrook Precision Manufacturing, Inc. The review form, the PIP and the EEO policy use Vanguard Precision Manufacturing, Inc. The termination memo uses Valiant Precision Manufacturing, Inc. The emails use a vanguardprecision.com domain. The draft uses Saxonbrook. Confirm the legal name, because the exhibits carry the other names.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Harmon&rsquo;s employee ID conflicts.<\/strong> Four records give four different IDs, ending in 0247, 0284, 0847 and 1087. The draft cites the 0247 form in CA-5 (cards 0028, 0042, 0046, 0054).<\/li>\n<li><strong>The Sellers coaching-plan date conflicts.<\/strong> The investigation summary dates his coaching plan to July 2024. The termination-log note gives 11/01/2024. The draft says only &ldquo;during 2024&rdquo; (cards 0047, 0054).<\/li>\n<li><strong>The date may be stale.<\/strong> The letter is dated April 18, 2025, the due date in the transmittal. The notes are dated 2026-10-05, and today is 2026-10-22. The record does not show the agent considering whether the deadline still applies or what date the letter should carry.<\/li>\n<li><strong>The exhibits are an index only.<\/strong> The draft lists Exhibits A&ndash;J and CA-1 to CA-5. The record shows no exhibit documents attached to the single .docx. CA-5 says VPM &ldquo;will produce&rdquo; its records on request, which is a commitment the company must be willing to make.<\/li>\n<li><strong>The closing message overstates coverage.<\/strong> It says &ldquo;All 10 source files read in full&rdquo; and that every date, figure, name and quote traces to a source. The workspace held 11 files. Several reads were cut off by the tool, so some sources were read in several passes, and the EEO policy&rsquo;s retaliation section was read separately (cards 0052, 0055, 0056). The traced items are the spot checks the agent ran, not every statement in the draft.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Several choices were made without asking.<\/strong> These include the respondent name, the employee ID, the Sellers date, the choice of the Summary Statistics tally over the Replacement Hires tab, and the framing of the investigation as thorough and in good faith (card_0059). The closing message mentions some of these but not the backfill conflict.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Environment changes.<\/strong> The agent installed python-docx and openpyxl with pip, created <code>process/notes.md<\/code> in the workspace, and left the .docx in place.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h2>Where the main claims come from<\/h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Claim<\/th><th>Where in the record<\/th><th>Status<\/th><\/tr><\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Harmon hired Aug 19, 2019; final salary $94,500; grade E-7<\/td><td>card_0046 (termination log)<\/td><td>Visible in source output<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>PIP Oct 14, 2024 to Jan 12, 2025; terminated Jan 17, 2025; 3 of 4 objectives missed<\/td><td>card_0046<\/td><td>Visible in source output<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>FY2024 rating 2.4 on Sept 6, 2024<\/td><td>card_0030, card_0034<\/td><td>Visible in source output<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>NCR closure 21.7 days in review period; 14.8 days under PIP<\/td><td>card_0034; card_0048<\/td><td>Visible in source output<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Supervisor&rsquo;s Dec 18, 2023 email reports 23.4 days<\/td><td>card_0040, card_0076<\/td><td>Visible in source output<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Severance of $7,269.23<\/td><td>card_0013 (charge); card_0054<\/td><td>Visible in charge text; arithmetic checked<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>16 performance terminations 2022 to 2025<\/td><td>card_0046 (summary tab)<\/td><td>Visible in source output<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>13 completed backfills<\/td><td>card_0046 (Summary Statistics: 13) vs. card_0069 (Replacement Hires tab: 12 stated, 13 rows)<\/td><td><strong>Conflict<\/strong><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Company name and employee ID<\/td><td>cards 0016, 0024, 0028, 0038, 0040, 0052, 0042, 0046<\/td><td><strong>Conflict<\/strong> across sources<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Four allegations not substantiated; 7 individuals interviewed<\/td><td>card_0053, card_0054 (agent&rsquo;s notes)<\/td><td>Agent&rsquo;s notes; full summary not visible in the cards<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n\n<footer>\nGenerated from the workspace cards only. No source outside the workspace was consulted. The Word file and notes file described here were not inspected directly.\n<\/footer>\n\n<\/main>\n<\/body>\n<\/html>\n"}, "same_run": "harvey-labs--codex--employment-labor-draft-eeoc-position-statement"});
